insert into d8new values
(1, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'In volume-controlled ventilation (VC-CMV), which of the following is NOT set by the nurse or doctor but changes breath by breath with the patient''s lung compliance and airway resistance?', '["Peak inspiratory pressure", "Mandatory respiratory rate", "Inspiratory flow rate", "Tidal volume"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In volume control the clinician sets tidal volume, flow (or inspiratory time) and rate; the pressure needed to deliver that volume is the dependent variable. Peak pressure therefore rises when compliance falls (e.g. ARDS, pneumothorax) or resistance rises (secretions, bronchospasm).', 'Respiratory'),
(2, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A patient on pressure-controlled ventilation (inspiratory pressure 20 cmH₂O, PEEP 8) suddenly drops exhaled tidal volume from 450 mL to 280 mL. The settings are unchanged and the cuff is not leaking. The most likely cause is:', '["A trigger sensitivity set too high, causing auto-cycling", "Reduced compliance or raised resistance (e.g. mucus plug, bronchospasm)", "A fall in respiratory drive from increased sedation", "Improved lung compliance as alveoli are recruited by PEEP"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In pressure control the pressure is fixed and the tidal volume is the dependent variable. Volume falls when the lungs become stiffer or the airways narrower, so check for secretions, bronchospasm, tube kinking, pneumothorax or worsening lung disease. A rise in compliance would increase volume.', 'Respiratory'),
(3, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'Plateau pressure on a ventilated patient is obtained by:', '["An end-inspiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive", "Reading the highest pressure reached during a normal breath", "Reading the airway pressure at the very start of inspiration", "An end-expiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive"]'::jsonb, 0, 'During an end-inspiratory pause there is no flow, so the pressure reflects alveolar (elastic) pressure only: the plateau pressure. An end-expiratory hold measures total PEEP (auto-PEEP). The highest pressure during a normal breath is the peak pressure.', 'Respiratory'),
(4, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient has a peak airway pressure of 45 cmH₂O and a plateau pressure of 20 cmH₂O. The most likely problem is:', '["Decreased lung compliance (ARDS, pulmonary oedema, fibrosis)", "Increased airway resistance (bronchospasm, secretions, biting)", "Endobronchial intubation into the right main bronchus", "Tension pneumothorax with mediastinal shift"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A large gap between peak and plateau pressure (here 25 cmH₂O) means pressure is being used to overcome resistance in the tube and airways. Problems of compliance — ARDS, oedema, pneumothorax, endobronchial intubation — raise BOTH peak and plateau pressures.', 'Respiratory'),
(5, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 500 mL, a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O and a PEEP of 10 cmH₂O. Static lung compliance is:', '["50 mL/cmH₂O", "12.5 mL/cmH₂O", "25 mL/cmH₂O", "16.7 mL/cmH₂O"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Static compliance = tidal volume ÷ (plateau pressure − PEEP) = 500 ÷ (30 − 10) = 25 mL/cmH₂O. Normal is about 50–100 mL/cmH₂O; values this low are typical of ARDS.', 'Respiratory'),
(6, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'Driving pressure during lung-protective ventilation is calculated as, and should ideally be kept below:', '["Peak pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O", "Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O", "Peak pressure minus plateau pressure; below about 15 cmH₂O", "Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 15 cmH₂O"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Driving pressure (Pplat − PEEP) reflects the strain applied to the ventilated lung; values above about 15 cmH₂O are associated with higher mortality in ARDS. Plateau pressure itself should stay at or below 30 cmH₂O.', 'Respiratory'),
(7, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A man 170 cm tall who weighs 90 kg develops ARDS. Using predicted body weight (male PBW = 50 + 0.91 × [height in cm − 152.4]), his initial tidal volume at 6 mL/kg is closest to:', '["330 mL", "540 mL", "400 mL", "480 mL"]'::jsonb, 2, 'PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (170 − 152.4) = 50 + 16 = 66 kg. 6 mL/kg × 66 kg ≈ 396 mL, so about 400 mL. Using actual weight (90 kg) would give 540 mL and over-distend the lungs; 480 mL is 8 mL/kg PBW.', 'Respiratory'),
(8, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'Which ventilator waveform finding indicates air trapping (auto-PEEP)?', '["The expiratory flow returns to zero and stays flat before the next breath", "The inspiratory flow pattern is square (constant) in volume control", "The pressure–time curve shows a flat plateau during an inspiratory pause", "The expiratory flow does not return to zero before the next breath begins"]'::jsonb, 3, 'If expiration is still going on when the next breath starts, gas is trapped in the lungs and pressure builds up (intrinsic PEEP). It is common in asthma and COPD, causes hypotension and barotrauma, and is confirmed with an end-expiratory hold.', 'Respiratory'),
(9, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient with severe asthma has rising auto-PEEP and falling blood pressure. The most effective ventilator change is to:', '["Raise the rate to wash out the retained CO₂ more quickly", "Lengthen inspiration by changing I:E from 1:3 to 1:1", "Lower the rate to lengthen expiration, accepting a higher PaCO₂", "Raise the tidal volume to improve alveolar ventilation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Air trapping is treated by giving more time to breathe out: lower rate, smaller tidal volume, higher inspiratory flow (shorter inspiration) and an I:E of 1:4 or more. Raising rate or tidal volume, or lengthening inspiration, worsens trapping. If the patient is shocked, briefly disconnect to let trapped gas escape.', 'Respiratory'),
(10, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient suddenly triggers the high-pressure alarm. SpO₂ is falling, BP is 70/40 mmHg, breath sounds are absent on the left, and the trachea is deviated to the right. The immediate action is:', '["Disconnect, bag with 100% oxygen, and wait for an urgent chest X-ray before acting", "Needle decompression of the left chest, followed by a chest drain", "Pass a suction catheter to clear a possible tube blockage", "Withdraw the endotracheal tube by 2 cm"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a tension pneumothorax with cardiovascular collapse. It is a clinical diagnosis and must be decompressed immediately (2nd intercostal space mid-clavicular line, or 4th/5th space anterior to mid-axillary line), not after an X-ray. Withdrawing the tube treats right main bronchus intubation, where the trachea is not deviated.', 'Respiratory'),
(11, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'The low-pressure and low exhaled-minute-volume alarms sound together, and the patient can be heard making a ''gurgling'' sound and vocalising around the tube. The most likely cause is:', '["The patient biting down on the tube during agitation", "A cuff leak or a tube that has slipped up above the cords", "Severe bronchospasm with reduced exhaled volumes", "A mucus plug partly blocking the endotracheal tube"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A leak lets gas escape, so pressure and exhaled volume fall, and air passing the cords produces sounds or a voice. Mucus plugs, bronchospasm and biting cause HIGH-pressure alarms. Check the tube position at the teeth or lips and the cuff pressure.', 'Respiratory'),
(12, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'The recommended endotracheal tube cuff pressure for an adult is:', '["25–35 mmHg", "20–30 cmH₂O", "10–15 cmH₂O", "30–40 cmH₂O"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Below about 20 cmH₂O, secretions leak around the cuff (VAP risk); above about 30 cmH₂O (≈22 mmHg), tracheal mucosal perfusion is impaired, risking ischaemia, stenosis and fistula. Measure with a manometer every shift — note the units are cmH₂O, not mmHg.', 'Respiratory'),
(13, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'The main reason PEEP improves oxygenation in ARDS is that it:', '["It keeps alveoli open at end-expiration, raising FRC and reducing shunt", "It raises minute ventilation, so more CO₂ is cleared each breath", "It reduces the inspiratory effort needed to trigger each breath", "It lowers intrathoracic pressure, improving venous return"]'::jsonb, 0, 'PEEP prevents alveolar collapse at the end of expiration, increasing FRC and the area for gas exchange and reducing intrapulmonary shunt. Its side effects come from RAISING intrathoracic pressure: reduced venous return and cardiac output, hypotension and barotrauma.', 'Respiratory'),
(14, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient has PaO₂ 55 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6 and PEEP 8 cmH₂O, with PaCO₂ 40 mmHg and pH 7.40. Which change will most directly improve oxygenation?', '["Increase the PEEP stepwise (with FiO₂ as needed)", "Shorten the inspiratory time", "Increase the respiratory rate", "Increase the tidal volume to 10 mL/kg"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Oxygenation depends mainly on FiO₂ and mean airway pressure (PEEP, inspiratory time). CO₂ removal depends on minute ventilation (rate × tidal volume). PaCO₂ is already normal, so increasing rate or volume adds nothing and a 10 mL/kg tidal volume causes volutrauma.', 'Respiratory'),
(15, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A ventilated patient has a PaCO₂ of 60 mmHg at a set rate of 14 breaths/min. Keeping the tidal volume the same, what rate is needed to bring PaCO₂ to 40 mmHg?', '["18 breaths/min", "21 breaths/min", "28 breaths/min", "9 breaths/min"]'::jsonb, 1, 'PaCO₂ is inversely proportional to alveolar ventilation: new rate = current rate × current PaCO₂ ÷ desired PaCO₂ = 14 × 60 ÷ 40 = 21 breaths/min.', 'Respiratory'),
(16, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'Which mode delivers a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised to the patient''s effort, while allowing spontaneous breaths in between at the patient''s own tidal volume (usually pressure-supported)?', '["Pressure support ventilation (PSV)", "Assist-control ventilation (A/C)", "Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP)", "Synchronised intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In SIMV, breaths above the set rate are the patient''s own spontaneous (supported) breaths. In assist-control, EVERY triggered breath is a full mandatory breath. PSV and CPAP have no mandatory breaths.', 'Respiratory'),
(17, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'In pressure support ventilation (PSV), the ventilator ends inspiration (cycles to expiration) when:', '["The preset tidal volume for that breath has been delivered", "Inspiratory flow falls to a set fraction of peak flow (e.g. 25%)", "Airway pressure rises to reach the high-pressure alarm limit", "The preset inspiratory time for that breath has elapsed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'PSV is patient-triggered, pressure-limited and FLOW-cycled. When the patient''s inspiratory flow decays to the cycle threshold (e.g. 25% of peak), the breath ends. Volume cycling belongs to volume control; time cycling to pressure control.', 'Respiratory'),
(18, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'The ventilator is delivering breaths without any patient effort (auto-triggering). The most common cause is:', '["A circuit leak or water in the tubing with a very sensitive trigger", "A trigger set too insensitive, so efforts are missed", "Deep sedation causing the ventilator to compensate", "Severe metabolic acidosis driving genuine extra breaths"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Leaks and water oscillating in the tubing create small flow or pressure changes that a sensitive trigger mistakes for patient effort, causing hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis. A raised drive from acidosis produces genuine triggering, not auto-triggering.', 'Respiratory'),
(19, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 30 breaths/min with a tidal volume of 250 mL. The rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI) and its interpretation are:', '["7.5 — weaning is likely to succeed", "120 — weaning is likely to fail", "83 — weaning is likely to succeed", "120 — weaning is likely to succeed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RSBI = respiratory rate ÷ tidal volume in litres = 30 ÷ 0.25 = 120 breaths/min/L. Values below 105 predict successful weaning; above 105 predict failure.', 'Respiratory'),
(20, 'Mechanical Ventilation: Parts, Set-up, Modes, Parameters and Alarms', 'A heat and moisture exchanger (HME) is in use on a ventilated patient. It should NOT be used together with:', '["A mainstream capnography adaptor", "A bacterial/viral filter on the expiratory limb", "An active heated humidifier in the same circuit", "An in-line closed suction catheter"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Using an HME and a heated humidifier together over-wets the HME, greatly increasing resistance and risking tube occlusion. HMEs are also avoided with very thick, copious or bloody secretions, large leaks, and hypothermia.', 'Respiratory'),
(21, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A syringe pump running noradrenaline is mounted high above the patient''s heart, and the syringe plunger is not secured in the plunger clamp. The main danger is:', '["Siphoning: free flow of drug by gravity, giving a bolus", "Repeated occlusion alarms with no drug being delivered", "Reverse flow of blood up the line into the syringe", "Air embolism from air drawn back into the syringe"]'::jsonb, 0, 'If the plunger and barrel are not held by the clamps and the syringe is above the patient, the contents can empty by gravity (siphonage), delivering a dangerous vasopressor bolus. Keep the pump at patient level, use anti-siphon lines, and seat the syringe correctly.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(22, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A syringe pump alarms ''occlusion'' because a three-way tap below it was closed. Before simply opening the tap, the nurse should:', '["Flush the line rapidly with 10 mL saline to clear any clot", "Replace the syringe, as the drug has now been inactivated", "Increase the rate briefly to make up for the missed drug", "Relieve the built-up line pressure first, to avoid a sudden bolus"]'::jsonb, 3, 'While occluded, the pump keeps pushing and pressurises the syringe and tubing. Opening the tap releases this stored volume as a bolus — dangerous with vasopressors, insulin or opioids. Many pumps have an automatic back-off after occlusion; otherwise relieve the pressure safely first.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(23, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'Which is the safest way to change an almost-empty syringe of noradrenaline for a patient who is pressor-dependent?', '["Stop the infusion, flush the line with saline, then connect the new syringe", "Run the new syringe through the lumen used for intermittent drug boluses", "Wait for the ''syringe empty'' alarm, then draw up and start the next syringe", "Prime a new syringe in a second pump early and switch over, watching the BP"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Interrupting a vasopressor causes sudden hypotension; flushing the line pushes the drug in the dead space as a bolus, causing a hypertensive surge. Use a dedicated lumen, prepare the new syringe early, and overlap or switch carefully with continuous arterial monitoring.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(24, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'Noradrenaline 4 mg is diluted to 50 mL. What rate (mL/h) delivers 0.1 microgram/kg/min to a 70 kg patient?', '["5.25 mL/h", "8.4 mL/h", "0.53 mL/h", "52.5 mL/h"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Concentration = 4,000 mcg ÷ 50 mL = 80 mcg/mL. Dose = 0.1 × 70 = 7 mcg/min = 420 mcg/h. Rate = 420 ÷ 80 = 5.25 mL/h.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(25, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'Dopamine 200 mg in 50 mL is running at 6 mL/h in an 80 kg patient. What dose is the patient receiving?', '["3 micrograms/kg/min", "50 micrograms/kg/min", "0.5 micrograms/kg/min", "5 micrograms/kg/min"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Concentration = 200,000 mcg ÷ 50 mL = 4,000 mcg/mL. 6 mL/h × 4,000 = 24,000 mcg/h = 400 mcg/min. 400 ÷ 80 kg = 5 mcg/kg/min.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(26, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'One litre of normal saline is to run over 8 hours through a giving set that delivers 20 drops/mL. The drip rate is:', '["42 drops/min", "125 drops/min", "21 drops/min", "63 drops/min"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Drops/min = volume (mL) × drop factor ÷ time (min) = 1,000 × 20 ÷ 480 = 41.7, rounded to 42 drops/min. (125 is the rate in mL/h.)', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(27, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A patient rescued from a closed room with a charcoal stove is confused, with a pulse oximeter reading of 98%. The reading is unreliable because:', '["Vasodilation from carbon monoxide improves the oximeter signal", "Carbon monoxide shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the right, raising SpO₂", "Standard pulse oximeters read carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin, giving a falsely normal SpO₂", "Carbon monoxide increases the amount of oxygen dissolved in plasma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two-wavelength oximeters cannot distinguish COHb from O₂Hb, so SpO₂ looks normal despite severe hypoxia. CO shifts the curve to the LEFT, impairing oxygen release to tissues. Measure COHb by CO-oximetry and give 100% oxygen.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(28, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'After a large dose of a local anaesthetic spray (benzocaine), a patient becomes cyanosed. SpO₂ stays around 85% despite high-flow oxygen, PaO₂ on the blood gas is 300 mmHg, and the blood looks chocolate-brown. The diagnosis and treatment are:', '["Carbon monoxide poisoning — hyperbaric oxygen", "Sulfhaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue", "Cyanide poisoning — hydroxocobalamin", "Methaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Oxidising drugs (benzocaine, dapsone, nitrates) convert haemoglobin to methaemoglobin, which cannot carry oxygen; SpO₂ typically settles near 85% while PaO₂ is normal or high (''saturation gap''). Treat with methylene blue 1–2 mg/kg. Sulfhaemoglobinaemia does NOT respond to methylene blue.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(29, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'Immediately after intubation, the capnograph shows a few small waveforms that fade to zero over the next five breaths. The most likely explanation is:', '["Severe bronchospasm", "Right main bronchus intubation", "Oesophageal intubation", "Hyperventilation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gas swallowed or bagged into the stomach can produce a few CO₂ traces that then disappear. Sustained waveform capnography over several breaths is the gold standard for confirming tracheal placement. Endobronchial intubation and bronchospasm still give persistent CO₂ traces.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(30, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A ''shark-fin'' capnograph waveform, with a slow sloping upstroke and no flat plateau, indicates:', '["Cardiac arrest with low pulmonary flow", "Lower airway obstruction (bronchospasm or COPD)", "Oesophageal intubation with gastric gas", "Rebreathing from exhausted soda lime"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Uneven, slow emptying of obstructed airways makes the CO₂ rise gradually, giving the shark-fin shape. Rebreathing raises the baseline above zero; oesophageal intubation gives no sustained waveform; arrest gives a very low or absent trace.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(31, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'On capnography, the baseline does not return to zero during inspiration (inspired CO₂ is above zero). This indicates:', '["Rebreathing of exhaled CO₂", "Hypoventilation with CO₂ retention", "Malignant hyperthermia in its early phase", "Bronchospasm with a prolonged expiration"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Inspired gas should contain no CO₂. A raised baseline means exhaled CO₂ is being breathed back in. Hypoventilation and malignant hyperthermia raise END-TIDAL CO₂ but the baseline still returns to zero.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(32, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'When an unstable patient with fast atrial fibrillation is cardioverted, ''sync'' mode is used so that the shock is NOT delivered during:', '["The PR interval", "The T wave", "The P wave", "The R wave"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sync mode times the shock to the R wave. A shock falling on the T wave (R-on-T) can trigger ventricular fibrillation. Pulseless VT and VF are defibrillated unsynchronised because there is no organised QRS to sync to.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(33, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'During transcutaneous pacing, every pacing spike is followed by a wide QRS complex. The best way to confirm that the heart is actually pumping (mechanical capture) is to:', '["Feel the femoral pulse or look at the arterial line trace", "Feel the carotid pulse", "Increase the current by 50% above the capture threshold", "Check that each spike is followed by a QRS on the monitor"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A QRS after each spike shows electrical capture only. External pacing makes the chest and neck muscles jerk, which can be mistaken for a carotid pulse, so use the femoral pulse, an arterial trace or SpO₂ pleth. Electrical capture without a pulse is PEA, so start CPR.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(34, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'An arterial line transducer is accidentally left 10 cm BELOW the phlebostatic axis. The displayed blood pressure will be:', '["Unchanged, because the system was zeroed earlier", "Falsely low, by about 7 mmHg", "Falsely high, by about 7 mmHg", "Falsely high, by about 10 mmHg"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A transducer below the heart has an extra column of fluid pressing on it, so readings are falsely high. 10 cmH₂O ≈ 7.4 mmHg (1 mmHg = 1.36 cmH₂O). A transducer above the heart reads falsely low. Re-level whenever the patient''s position changes.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(35, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'An arterial line trace is flattened, with a slurred upstroke and no dicrotic notch (over-damped). Compared with the true pressure, the monitor shows:', '["Falsely high systolic and diastolic, with a falsely high mean", "Accurate systolic and diastolic, but a mean 20 mmHg too low", "Falsely LOW systolic and falsely HIGH diastolic, mean about right", "Falsely HIGH systolic and falsely LOW diastolic, mean about right"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Over-damping (air bubbles, clots, kinks, soft tubing, low flush-bag pressure) blunts the waveform: systolic is under-read, diastolic over-read, MAP stays close. Under-damping (resonance) does the opposite — overshooting systolic, undershooting diastolic.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(36, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A fast-flush (square-wave) test on an arterial line shows 1½–2 oscillations before the trace returns to the waveform. This indicates:', '["An optimally damped system", "A disconnected transducer", "An under-damped system", "An over-damped system"]'::jsonb, 0, 'After the flush, an optimally damped system rings 1.5–2 times then settles. Fewer than 1.5 oscillations with a slow return means over-damping; more than 2–3 oscillations means under-damping.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(37, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A patient has an underwater-seal chest drain for a pneumothorax. The water-seal chamber is bubbling CONTINUOUSLY, during both inspiration and expiration. This means:', '["The drain tube is blocked by a clot or kinked under the patient", "A persistent air leak — from the lung or a loose connection", "A normal finding in every drain inserted for a pneumothorax", "The lung has fully re-expanded and the drain can be removed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bubbling with expiration or coughing is expected while a pneumothorax drains. CONTINUOUS bubbling means air is constantly entering — from the lung, or through a loose connection or a drain hole outside the chest. Check the connections from the patient downwards. A blocked drain or a re-expanded lung stops bubbling and swinging.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(38, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A patient''s chest drain bottle is accidentally lifted above the level of the chest. The main risk is:', '["Backflow of drained fluid into the pleural space", "Re-expansion pulmonary oedema of the lung", "Tension pneumothorax because air cannot escape", "Air embolism entering the pulmonary veins"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The water seal relies on gravity. If the bottle is higher than the chest, fluid can siphon back into the pleural cavity, risking infection and re-accumulation. Always keep the bottle upright and below chest level. Clamping is NOT routine, as it can cause tension pneumothorax.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(39, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'During continuous renal replacement therapy with regional citrate anticoagulation, the patient''s total calcium is rising while ionised calcium falls (total:ionised calcium ratio above 2.5), with a worsening metabolic acidosis. This indicates:', '["Hypercalcaemia from excessive calcium replacement", "Citrate accumulation, as the liver cannot clear citrate", "Severe hypoalbuminaemia lowering the total calcium", "Early clotting of the filter and extracorporeal circuit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Citrate binds ionised calcium. If the liver cannot metabolise citrate (liver failure, severe shock), citrate–calcium complexes build up, so total calcium rises while ionised calcium falls and an anion-gap acidosis develops. Reduce or stop the citrate and review calcium replacement.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(40, 'ICU Machines and Equipment: Syringe Pumps, Monitors, Defibrillator, Drains, CRRT, Oxygen Devices', 'A Venturi mask delivers a fixed, predictable FiO₂ mainly because:', '["Its tight face seal stops room air entering, so inspired gas comes only from the device", "Its reservoir bag stores pure oxygen so that each breath contains a fixed oxygen fraction", "Turning the oxygen flow up to 15 L/min raises the FiO₂ above the colour-coded value", "It entrains a fixed ratio of room air, giving a total flow above the patient''s peak inspiratory flow"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Each colour-coded Venturi valve entrains a set ratio of air (the Bernoulli/Venturi effect), producing a high total flow so the patient does not draw extra room air. FiO₂ is therefore stable — ideal for COPD with CO₂ retention. Turning the oxygen flow higher does not raise the FiO₂ beyond the valve''s value.', 'ICU Pharmacology'),
(41, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'Six hours after cardiac surgery, a patient''s BP falls to 80/60 mmHg. Heart rate is 125, JVP is raised, heart sounds are muffled, systolic pressure drops 15 mmHg on inspiration, and chest drain output has suddenly stopped. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Hypovolaemia from concealed bleeding", "Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Right ventricular infarction"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Beck''s triad (hypotension, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds) plus pulsus paradoxus and a sudden drop in drain output (clot blocking the drains) point to tamponade. Tension pneumothorax gives absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation; hypovolaemia gives a LOW JVP; RV infarction does not muffle heart sounds.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(42, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A patient with an inferior STEMI becomes hypotensive (80/50 mmHg) with a raised JVP and CLEAR lung fields. ST elevation is seen in right-sided lead V4R. The diagnosis and key management are:', '["Massive PE — systemic thrombolysis immediately", "RV infarction — IV fluid boluses; avoid nitrates", "Cardiac tamponade — urgent pericardiocentesis", "LV failure — IV furosemide and GTN infusion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'RV infarction (usually right coronary artery) makes cardiac output preload-dependent: hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs, ST elevation in V4R. Nitrates, diuretics and morphine reduce preload and can cause profound hypotension; give cautious fluid boluses and arrange reperfusion.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(43, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 58-year-old hypertensive man has sudden ''tearing'' chest pain radiating to the back. BP is 180/100 mmHg in the right arm and 140/80 mmHg in the left arm, and there is a new early diastolic murmur. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Massive pulmonary thromboembolism", "Acute anterior myocardial infarction", "Stanford type A aortic dissection", "Acute pericarditis with effusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Tearing pain to the back, an inter-arm BP difference above 20 mmHg, and aortic regurgitation (early diastolic murmur) indicate ascending aortic dissection — a surgical emergency. Thrombolysis or anticoagulation would be catastrophic. Control heart rate and BP (e.g. IV labetalol) and refer for surgery.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(44, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 30-year-old has sharp central chest pain that is worse lying flat and relieved by sitting forward. The ECG shows widespread concave (''saddle-shaped'') ST elevation with PR depression, and a scratchy sound is heard at the left sternal edge. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute pericarditis", "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy", "Benign early repolarisation", "Acute anterior STEMI"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Positional pleuritic pain, a pericardial friction rub, diffuse concave ST elevation not confined to one coronary territory, and PR depression are classic for pericarditis. STEMI gives territorial convex ST elevation with reciprocal depression.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(45, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'Five days after an acute MI, a patient suddenly develops severe breathlessness and pink frothy sputum, with a NEW loud pansystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. The most likely complication is:', '["Papillary muscle rupture (acute mitral regurgitation)", "Ventricular septal rupture (acute left-to-right shunt)", "Dressler syndrome (post-MI autoimmune pericarditis)", "Left ventricular free wall rupture with tamponade"]'::jsonb, 0, 'An apical pansystolic murmur radiating to the axilla with sudden pulmonary oedema = acute mitral regurgitation from papillary muscle rupture (often after inferior MI). VSD gives a pansystolic murmur at the LEFT STERNAL EDGE with a step-up in oxygen saturation in the RV; free wall rupture causes tamponade and PEA.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(46, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'Three weeks after an MI, a patient develops fever, pleuritic chest pain, a pericardial rub and a raised ESR. Echo shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality. The diagnosis is:', '["Infective endocarditis", "Left ventricular aneurysm", "Dressler syndrome", "Ventricular free wall rupture"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Dressler syndrome is an immune-mediated pericarditis appearing weeks after MI, with fever, pleuritic pain, rub and raised inflammatory markers. Free wall rupture occurs in the first days and causes tamponade and collapse; aneurysm gives persistent ST elevation and heart failure.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(47, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'An injecting drug user has fever for 2 weeks, a new pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge that increases on inspiration, splinter haemorrhages, painless red macules on the soles, and multiple round opacities on chest X-ray. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Tricuspid valve endocarditis with septic emboli", "Community-acquired pneumonia with lung abscess", "Mitral valve endocarditis with cerebral emboli", "Acute rheumatic fever with carditis and arthritis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'IV drug use typically infects the tricuspid valve (Staphylococcus aureus). A tricuspid regurgitation murmur louder on inspiration (Carvallo''s sign), Janeway lesions, splinter haemorrhages and septic pulmonary emboli fit. Take three sets of blood cultures before antibiotics.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(48, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A patient''s BP is 230/130 mmHg with severe headache, confusion, papilloedema and new proteinuria. The appropriate initial target for blood pressure reduction is:', '["Give sublingual nifedipine to bring the BP down as fast as possible", "Lower MAP by no more than about 25% in the first hour with an IV agent", "Bring the BP down to below 140/90 mmHg within the first hour", "Lower the systolic BP to 120 mmHg within 6 hours with an IV agent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is a hypertensive emergency (encephalopathy). Cerebral autoregulation has reset upward, so rapid normalisation causes cerebral, coronary and renal ischaemia. Reduce MAP by at most 20–25% in the first hour, then gradually towards 160/100 over the next 2–6 hours, with IV labetalol, nicardipine or GTN. Aortic dissection is the exception: it needs a rapid fall.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(49, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 22-year-old has palpitations with an irregular, very fast (rate 260) broad-complex tachycardia whose QRS shape varies from beat to beat. A previous ECG showed a short PR interval and a slurred upstroke (delta wave). Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED?', '["Flecainide", "Verapamil", "Synchronised DC cardioversion", "Procainamide"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is atrial fibrillation conducted down an accessory pathway (Wolff–Parkinson–White). Blocking the AV node with verapamil, diltiazem, adenosine or digoxin pushes more impulses down the accessory pathway and can cause VF. Use synchronised cardioversion if unstable, or a drug that slows accessory-pathway conduction (procainamide, flecainide).', 'Cardiovascular'),
(50, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A patient with hypomagnesaemia who is taking a QT-prolonging drug develops a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS axis appears to twist around the baseline. A pulse is present. The first-line drug is:', '["IV magnesium sulphate 2 g", "IV adenosine 6 mg", "IV amiodarone 300 mg", "IV lidocaine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Torsades de pointes is linked to a long QT (hypomagnesaemia, hypokalaemia, drugs). IV magnesium is first-line even if the magnesium level is normal; correct potassium and stop the offending drugs. Amiodarone prolongs QT further. Pulseless torsades is defibrillated.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(51, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'An elderly man has dizzy spells. The pulse is 34/min and regular. On the ECG, P waves occur at 80/min, QRS complexes at 34/min, there is no consistent PR interval, and the QRS complexes are wide. The rhythm is:', '["Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach)", "Sinus bradycardia", "Third-degree (complete) heart block", "Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Complete AV dissociation (P waves and QRS complexes ''marching'' independently) with a slow, wide ventricular escape rhythm is third-degree block. It needs atropine or an infusion as a bridge, then transcutaneous or transvenous pacing and a permanent pacemaker. In Mobitz II, some P waves ARE conducted with a constant PR.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(52, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'An ECG shows a constant PR interval of 0.20 s. Every few beats, a P wave is suddenly not followed by a QRS, without any progressive PR lengthening beforehand. This rhythm is:', '["Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block — usually benign", "First-degree AV block with intermittent sinus arrest — usually benign", "Mobitz type II second-degree AV block — high risk of complete block", "Third-degree (complete) heart block with a junctional escape rhythm"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Mobitz II has dropped beats with a FIXED PR interval and is usually infranodal, so it may suddenly progress to complete block or asystole; it needs pacing. Mobitz I shows progressive PR lengthening until a beat drops, and is often benign.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(53, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 19-year-old athlete faints during a football match. A harsh ejection systolic murmur at the left sternal edge gets LOUDER on standing and during the Valsalva manoeuvre, and softer on squatting. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy", "Dilated cardiomyopathy with low output", "Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation", "Calcific aortic valve stenosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In HOCM, outflow obstruction INCREASES when LV filling falls (standing, Valsalva, dehydration), so the murmur gets louder. The aortic stenosis murmur gets softer with Valsalva. HOCM is a leading cause of sudden cardiac death in young athletes; avoid dehydration and vasodilators.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(54, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A breathless patient has bilateral lung infiltrates and a PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio of 150, an S3 gallop, raised BNP and a pulmonary artery wedge pressure of 28 mmHg. The most likely cause of the infiltrates is:', '["Transfusion-related acute lung injury", "Bilateral bacterial pneumonia", "Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary oedema", "Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A high PAWP (above 18 mmHg), S3 and raised BNP mean the oedema is hydrostatic, from left heart failure. ARDS and TRALI are non-cardiogenic: the PAWP is normal and the infiltrates are not explained by heart failure or fluid overload. Treat with nitrates, diuretics and CPAP.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(55, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'Five days after a hip replacement, a patient suddenly becomes breathless with pleuritic pain. HR is 130, BP 75/50 mmHg, JVP raised, lungs clear, and echo shows a dilated, poorly contracting right ventricle. The most likely diagnosis and treatment are:', '["Massive pulmonary embolism — systemic thrombolysis", "Right ventricular infarction — IV fluids only", "Cardiac tamponade — pericardiocentesis", "Tension pneumothorax — needle decompression"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sudden collapse with RV dilatation, a raised JVP and clear lungs in a high-risk post-operative patient means massive (high-risk) PE. With shock, systemic thrombolysis (e.g. alteplase) is indicated unless contraindicated; otherwise use surgical or catheter embolectomy.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(56, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A patient in shock has cardiac index 1.6 L/min/m², PAWP 24 mmHg, SVR 1,800 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ and cold, clammy peripheries. The type of shock is:', '["Hypovolaemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Obstructive shock from massive PE", "Septic (distributive) shock"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Low cardiac output + HIGH filling pressure (PAWP) + high SVR = pump failure. Hypovolaemic shock has low CO, LOW PAWP and high SVR; septic shock has low SVR and usually high CO; massive PE has a high CVP but a normal or low PAWP.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(57, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 72-year-old smoker with hypertension has sudden severe back and left flank pain and collapses. BP is 70/40 mmHg, and a tender pulsatile mass is felt in the epigastrium. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Acute pancreatitis", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Left renal colic"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The triad of pain (abdomen, back or flank), hypotension and a pulsatile abdominal mass in an older male smoker is a ruptured AAA until proven otherwise. It is often misdiagnosed as renal colic. Use permissive hypotension (systolic around 70–90 mmHg) and arrange immediate vascular surgery.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(58, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'A 65-year-old woman develops chest pain and anterior ST elevation a few hours after hearing that her son has died. Coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries, and the left ventricle is ballooned at the apex with a hypercontractile base. The diagnosis is:', '["Anterior STEMI from LAD occlusion", "Takotsubo (stress-induced) cardiomyopathy", "Acute pericarditis", "Acute viral myocarditis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Takotsubo cardiomyopathy follows emotional or physical stress, mostly in postmenopausal women. It mimics STEMI but the coronaries are unobstructed, with typical apical ballooning. LV function usually recovers within weeks.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(59, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'An elderly patient on digoxin and furosemide has nausea, vomiting, confusion, yellow-green tinted vision, a slow irregular pulse and frequent ventricular ectopics (bigeminy). Potassium is 2.9 mmol/L. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Digoxin toxicity, made worse by hypokalaemia", "Beta-blocker overdose", "Hyperkalaemia from renal failure", "Hypercalcaemia"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hypokalaemia (from loop diuretics) increases digoxin binding to the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase and promotes toxicity: GI upset, visual disturbance (xanthopsia), confusion and arrhythmias. Stop digoxin, correct potassium and magnesium, and give digoxin-specific antibody fragments for life-threatening arrhythmias.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(60, 'Identify the Disease: Cardiovascular', 'An ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF, with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. The culprit artery is most likely the:', '["Left main stem", "Left circumflex artery", "Left anterior descending artery", "Right coronary artery"]'::jsonb, 3, 'II, III and aVF view the inferior wall, supplied by the RCA in about 80% of people (right-dominant). Reciprocal changes in I and aVL and ST elevation in III greater than II also favour the RCA over the circumflex. Check V4R for RV involvement.', 'Cardiovascular'),
(61, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'An asthmatic patient is exhausted, with SpO₂ 91% on air, a silent chest, peak flow 28% of predicted and a PaCO₂ of 45 mmHg (6.0 kPa). This attack is best classified as:', '["Acute severe asthma", "Moderate exacerbation", "Life-threatening asthma", "Near-fatal asthma"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Life-threatening features (BTS) include PEF below 33%, SpO₂ below 92%, a silent chest, exhaustion and a NORMAL PaCO₂ (4.6–6.0 kPa), which means the patient is tiring. Near-fatal asthma means a RAISED PaCO₂ or needing ventilation. Acute severe asthma is PEF 33–50% with RR ≥25, HR ≥110 or inability to complete sentences.', 'Respiratory'),
(62, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient with COPD has pH 7.28, PaCO₂ 70 mmHg and HCO₃⁻ 32 mmol/L. This is best interpreted as:', '["Acute uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation", "Acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The PaCO₂ is 30 mmHg above normal. In acute retention, HCO₃⁻ rises only ~1 mmol/L per 10 mmHg (to ~27); with chronic compensation it rises ~3.5–4 per 10 (to ~34–36). A HCO₃⁻ of 32 with an acidaemic pH means chronic retention plus a fresh acute rise, an indication for NIV.', 'Respiratory'),
(63, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient''s PaO₂ is 75 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6 with PEEP 10 cmH₂O, and chest X-ray shows bilateral opacities within a week of severe pancreatitis, not explained by heart failure. By the Berlin definition this is:', '["Mild ARDS", "Severe ARDS", "Not ARDS, because the PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio must be below 100", "Moderate ARDS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'PaO₂/FiO₂ = 75 ÷ 0.6 = 125. Berlin (with PEEP ≥5 cmH₂O): mild 200–300, moderate 100–200, severe ≤100.', 'Respiratory'),
(64, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A tall, thin 20-year-old smoker has sudden left-sided pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness. On the left there is reduced chest expansion, HYPER-resonant percussion and reduced breath sounds; the trachea is central and BP is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Left lower lobe pneumonia", "Left lower lobe collapse", "Primary spontaneous pneumothorax", "Large left pleural effusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hyper-resonance with reduced breath sounds means air in the pleural space. A central trachea and normal BP mean it is not under tension. An effusion is ''stony dull''; consolidation and collapse are dull.', 'Respiratory'),
(65, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'On examination of the right chest: reduced expansion, STONY dull percussion, absent breath sounds, reduced vocal resonance, and the trachea is pushed to the LEFT. The most likely finding is:', '["Right tension pneumothorax", "Consolidation of the right lower lobe", "A large right pleural effusion", "Collapse of the right lung"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Stony dullness with absent breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance = fluid. A large effusion pushes the mediastinum AWAY. Collapse pulls the trachea TOWARDS the affected side; consolidation gives bronchial breathing and INCREASED vocal resonance; pneumothorax is hyper-resonant.', 'Respiratory'),
(66, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A post-operative patient with a weak cough has the trachea pulled to the RIGHT, dull percussion and reduced breath sounds over the right lower chest, and a raised right hemidiaphragm on X-ray. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Right-sided tension pneumothorax", "Right lower lobe collapse (atelectasis)", "Large right-sided pleural effusion", "Right lower lobe consolidation (pneumonia)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Volume loss PULLS the trachea and mediastinum towards the affected side and raises the hemidiaphragm. Treat with physiotherapy, suctioning and possibly bronchoscopy. An effusion pushes the trachea away; consolidation causes no volume loss.', 'Respiratory'),
(67, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A febrile patient has dull percussion, BRONCHIAL breathing, increased vocal resonance and coarse crackles over the right lower zone, with a central trachea. These signs indicate:', '["Pneumothorax", "Lobar collapse", "Pleural effusion", "Lobar consolidation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Consolidated lung transmits sound well, giving bronchial breathing and increased vocal resonance, with dullness but no shift of the mediastinum. Fluid (effusion) muffles sound and reduces vocal resonance.', 'Respiratory'),
(68, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A 60-year-old returned from a hotel stay with air-conditioning 5 days ago. He has high fever, dry cough, confusion, diarrhoea, Na⁺ 126 mmol/L and raised liver enzymes. The most likely organism is:', '["Klebsiella pneumoniae", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Legionella pneumophila"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Legionnaires'' disease spreads in contaminated water systems (air-conditioning, showers). It causes pneumonia with GI upset, confusion, hyponatraemia and deranged LFTs. Diagnose with the urinary antigen test; treat with a macrolide or fluoroquinolone (it does not respond to beta-lactams alone).', 'Respiratory'),
(69, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A malnourished alcoholic has high fever and cough with thick, dark ''redcurrant jelly'' sputum. Chest X-ray shows right upper lobe consolidation with cavitation and a bulging fissure. The most likely organism is:', '["Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Klebsiella pneumoniae"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Klebsiella classically affects alcoholics and diabetics, with redcurrant-jelly sputum, upper-lobe cavitation and a bulging fissure. Staph aureus pneumonia often follows influenza and forms thin-walled cavities and abscesses; TB is more chronic, with night sweats and weight loss.', 'Respiratory'),
(70, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient admitted with 2 months of cough, haemoptysis, night sweats and weight loss has upper-lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Until sputum results are back, the correct precaution is:', '["Droplet: single room and surgical masks within 1 metre", "Standard precautions only until a sputum smear is positive", "Airborne: negative-pressure room and N95/FFP3 respirators", "Contact: gloves and gown for any contact with the patient"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Suspected pulmonary TB spreads by airborne droplet nuclei that stay suspended for hours. Use airborne isolation (negative-pressure room, fitted N95/FFP3 respirators) and put a surgical mask on the patient during transport.', 'Respiratory'),
(71, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient with HIV (CD4 count 80 cells/µL) has 3 weeks of dry cough and fever and desaturates on walking. Chest X-ray shows bilateral perihilar ''ground-glass'' shadowing, and LDH is high. The most likely diagnosis and treatment are:', '["CMV pneumonitis — IV ganciclovir with CMV immunoglobulin", "Pneumocystis pneumonia — high-dose co-trimoxazole ± steroids", "Pneumococcal pneumonia — high-dose IV amoxicillin alone", "Pulmonary tuberculosis — rifampicin-based four-drug therapy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'PCP occurs with CD4 below 200: insidious dry cough, marked exertional desaturation, bilateral interstitial or ground-glass change (the X-ray can look nearly normal) and a raised LDH. Treat with co-trimoxazole and add prednisolone if PaO₂ is below 70 mmHg.', 'Respiratory'),
(72, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A drunk patient vomits and aspirates while lying flat on his back. The lung segment most likely to be affected is the:', '["Basal segments of the left lower lobe", "Superior (apical) segment of the right lower lobe", "Lingula of the left upper lobe", "Right middle lobe"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical. Lying SUPINE, aspirate runs to the posterior parts: the superior segment of the right lower lobe and the posterior segment of the right upper lobe. Upright aspiration favours the basal segments of the right lower lobe.', 'Respiratory'),
(73, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'Two days after a closed fracture of the femoral shaft, a young man becomes confused and hypoxaemic, with a petechial rash over the axillae, chest and conjunctivae. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Hypovolaemic shock from concealed bleeding", "Pulmonary thromboembolism", "Fat embolism syndrome", "Septic shock"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fat embolism typically appears 24–72 h after a long-bone or pelvic fracture with the triad of respiratory distress, neurological change and petechial rash. Early fixation of fractures reduces the risk. Treatment is supportive (oxygen or ventilation).', 'Respiratory'),
(74, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient with a known LEFT upper lobe cavity suddenly coughs up about 300 mL of fresh blood. While preparing for definitive airway management, the patient should be positioned:', '["Prone", "Flat on the back", "Lying on the RIGHT side (bleeding side up)", "Lying on the LEFT side (bleeding side down)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Lying with the bleeding lung DOWN uses gravity to keep blood in the diseased lung and protects the healthy lung''s gas exchange. If intubation is needed, the healthy lung may be selectively intubated or the bleeding side blocked.', 'Respiratory'),
(75, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A 65-year-old long-term smoker has FEV₁ 1.2 L and FVC 3.0 L (FEV₁/FVC 40%), with no significant improvement after a bronchodilator. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Asthma", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis", "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"]'::jsonb, 2, 'An FEV₁/FVC ratio below 0.7 that does not reverse after bronchodilator = fixed airflow obstruction (COPD). Asthma shows significant reversibility (above 12% and 200 mL). Fibrosis is restrictive, with a normal or HIGH ratio and a reduced FVC.', 'Respiratory'),
(76, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A 68-year-old has progressive breathlessness over 2 years, finger clubbing, and fine bibasal ''Velcro-like'' end-inspiratory crackles. Spirometry is restrictive and CT shows basal honeycombing. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Chronic heart failure", "Bronchiectasis", "COPD", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fine dry ''Velcro'' crackles, clubbing, restrictive spirometry with reduced gas transfer, and basal subpleural honeycombing on CT (usual interstitial pneumonia pattern) typify IPF. Bronchiectasis gives coarse crackles and copious purulent sputum.', 'Respiratory'),
(77, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A pleural tap shows pleural protein 40 g/L (serum 60 g/L) and pleural LDH 300 IU/L (serum 400 IU/L; upper limit of normal 220). By Light''s criteria the fluid is:', '["A transudate — e.g. cirrhosis", "A transudate — e.g. heart failure", "An exudate — e.g. parapneumonic effusion, malignancy or TB", "An empyema, because the protein is above 30 g/L"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Exudate if ANY of: pleural/serum protein above 0.5 (here 0.67), pleural/serum LDH above 0.6 (here 0.75), or pleural LDH above two-thirds of the serum upper limit of normal (here 300 > 147). Empyema is diagnosed by frank pus, a positive Gram stain or culture, or a pleural pH below 7.2.', 'Respiratory'),
(78, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A 35-year-old has a severe sore throat, fever, drooling, a muffled ''hot potato'' voice and soft inspiratory stridor. He sits leaning forward and cannot swallow his saliva. The most likely diagnosis and key nursing action are:', '["Acute asthma — salbutamol nebuliser with the patient lying flat", "Epiglottitis — keep upright, don''t examine the throat, call airway help", "Viral croup — nebulised adrenaline, oral dexamethasone, then discharge", "Quinsy — examine the throat with a tongue depressor and drain it"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Epiglottitis can cause sudden complete airway obstruction. Do not lay the patient flat or examine the throat; give oxygen as tolerated and get an anaesthetist/ENT to secure the airway, ideally in theatre. Quinsy causes trismus and a deviated uvula; croup has a barking cough in young children.', 'Respiratory'),
(79, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A 55-year-old on lisinopril develops rapidly increasing swelling of the lips and tongue, with no urticaria, no itch and no wheeze. BP is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Ludwig''s angina (floor-of-mouth sepsis)", "Anaphylaxis (IgE-mediated reaction)", "Hereditary C1-esterase angioedema", "ACE-inhibitor (bradykinin) angioedema"]'::jsonb, 3, 'ACE inhibitors stop bradykinin being broken down. The angioedema has no urticaria or itch, can occur after years of treatment, and responds poorly to adrenaline, antihistamines and steroids. Stop the ACE inhibitor and protect the airway early. Ludwig''s angina is a floor-of-mouth infection with fever.', 'Respiratory'),
(80, 'Identify the Disease: Respiratory', 'A patient with an exacerbation of COPD is alert, with RR 28, pH 7.30, PaCO₂ 62 mmHg and SpO₂ 86% after nebulisers and steroids. The most appropriate next step is:', '["Give 15 L/min via non-rebreather to keep SpO₂ above 98%", "Intubate and ventilate immediately in the emergency room", "Continue nebulisers only and repeat the gas in 12 hours", "Start NIV (BiPAP) with oxygen titrated to SpO₂ 88–92%"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Acute hypercapnic respiratory failure (pH 7.25–7.35, PaCO₂ above 45 mmHg) despite optimal medical therapy is the classic indication for NIV, which reduces intubation and mortality. High-flow oxygen worsens hypercapnia in CO₂ retainers. Intubate if NIV fails, or if the patient cannot protect the airway or has a pH below about 7.25 and is deteriorating.', 'Respiratory'),
(81, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'Two weeks after a bout of diarrhoea, a 30-year-old develops symmetrical weakness that started in the legs and is ascending, with absent reflexes and mild tingling. CSF shows protein 2.1 g/L and 2 white cells/µL. The diagnosis and the key nursing monitoring are:', '["Guillain–Barré syndrome — regular forced vital capacity checks", "Myasthenia gravis — regular checks of ptosis and diplopia", "Transverse myelitis — regular checks for a sensory level", "Botulism — regular checks of pupil size and reactivity"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Ascending areflexic weakness after Campylobacter infection with albuminocytological dissociation (high CSF protein, normal cells) is GBS. Respiratory failure and autonomic instability are the main killers; SpO₂ falls late, so FVC is the key measure. Treat with IV immunoglobulin or plasma exchange.', 'Neurology'),
(82, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient with myasthenia gravis took extra doses of pyridostigmine and now has worsening weakness with small pupils, sweating, excess saliva, abdominal cramps, diarrhoea and muscle fasciculations. The most likely problem is:', '["Guillain–Barré syndrome", "Cholinergic crisis", "Myasthenic crisis", "Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Excess acetylcholinesterase inhibitor causes muscarinic effects (miosis, salivation, sweating, diarrhoea, bradycardia) and nicotinic depolarising weakness with fasciculations. Myasthenic crisis (too little drug, infection, surgery) causes weakness WITHOUT these cholinergic signs. Both can need ventilation; stop the anticholinesterase in cholinergic crisis.', 'Neurology'),
(83, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'After eating home-canned food, a patient develops double vision, drooping eyelids, slurred speech and difficulty swallowing, followed by weakness spreading DOWNWARDS to the arms. The pupils are dilated and poorly reactive, and sensation is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Myasthenia gravis", "Botulism", "Guillain–Barré syndrome", "Miller Fisher syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Botulinum toxin blocks acetylcholine release, causing DESCENDING symmetrical paralysis that starts in the cranial nerves, with dilated, sluggish pupils and intact sensation. GBS ascends; myasthenia spares the pupils and is fatigable; Miller Fisher syndrome is ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and areflexia.', 'Neurology'),
(84, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient with a head injury has a left dilated pupil. Two hours later BP rises from 130/80 to 190/70 mmHg, the pulse falls from 90 to 48, and breathing becomes irregular. These changes represent:', '["Beck''s triad of cardiac tamponade", "Autonomic dysreflexia from a full bladder", "Neurogenic shock from cord injury", "Cushing''s triad of raised intracranial pressure"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Cushing''s response = hypertension with a WIDENING pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular breathing, as the brainstem is compressed. It is a pre-terminal sign needing immediate action (head up 30°, osmotherapy, neurosurgery). Neurogenic shock causes HYPOtension with bradycardia.', 'Neurology'),
(85, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly has a pounding headache, BP 210/110 mmHg, pulse 50, a flushed and sweaty face above the injury, and pale, cool skin below it. The FIRST nursing action is to:', '["Give IV atropine first to treat the slow heart rate", "Sit the patient up and check the bladder and catheter", "Give IV labetalol first, then look for the cause later", "Lay the patient flat with the legs raised to restore flow"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This is autonomic dysreflexia (injury at T6 or above): a noxious stimulus below the injury (most often a full bladder, then constipation or a pressure sore) triggers uncontrolled sympathetic vasoconstriction. Sit up to lower BP, loosen tight clothing, and remove the trigger. Give drugs only if BP stays high.', 'Neurology'),
(86, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'After a C5 spinal cord injury from a fall, a patient has BP 75/40 mmHg, pulse 48, and warm, dry, pink peripheries. The most likely type of shock is:', '["Neurogenic shock", "Hypovolaemic shock", "Septic shock", "Spinal shock"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Loss of sympathetic outflow (injury above T6) causes vasodilation (warm skin) and unopposed vagal tone (bradycardia). Hypovolaemia causes tachycardia and cold peripheries — rule out bleeding in trauma. ''Spinal shock'' is a temporary loss of reflexes below the injury, not a circulatory state.', 'Neurology'),
(87, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A young man is hit on the side of the head with a stick, briefly loses consciousness, then is lucid and talking for 2 hours before rapidly deteriorating with a dilated pupil on the side of the injury. CT shows a biconvex (lens-shaped) collection. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute subdural haematoma (bridging veins)", "Extradural haematoma (middle meningeal artery)", "Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Diffuse axonal injury from shearing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A temporal blow, a lucid interval, then rapid deterioration and a lens-shaped bleed limited by the skull sutures are typical of an arterial extradural haematoma. It needs emergency evacuation. An acute subdural (bridging veins) is crescent-shaped and crosses sutures.', 'Neurology'),
(88, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A 78-year-old alcoholic on warfarin has 3 weeks of fluctuating confusion, headache and drowsiness. He had a minor fall a month ago. CT shows a crescent-shaped collection that is darker (hypodense) than the brain. The diagnosis is:', '["Subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Chronic subdural haematoma", "Acute extradural haematoma", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Brain atrophy (age, alcohol) stretches the bridging veins, and anticoagulation adds risk. A minor injury can cause a slow venous bleed with fluctuating symptoms weeks later; old blood appears hypodense on CT. Reverse the anticoagulation and refer for burr-hole drainage.', 'Neurology'),
(89, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A 40-year-old has a sudden ''worst ever'' headache that reached maximum intensity within a minute, with neck stiffness and photophobia. A CT head 14 hours after onset is normal. The next investigation is:', '["MRI brain with gadolinium contrast", "Electroencephalogram to exclude seizure", "Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia", "Repeat non-contrast CT after 48 hours"]'::jsonb, 2, 'CT sensitivity for subarachnoid haemorrhage falls after 6 hours. A normal CT does not exclude SAH, so perform an LP at least 12 hours after the headache to detect xanthochromia (bilirubin from broken-down red cells), then CT angiography if positive.', 'Neurology'),
(90, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'Six days after clipping of a ruptured aneurysm (SAH), a patient becomes newly confused with a right-sided weakness. Repeat CT shows no rebleed and no hydrocephalus. The most likely cause, and the drug given from admission to reduce this risk, are:', '["Hydrocephalus — acetazolamide", "Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm — oral nimodipine", "Rebleeding — tranexamic acid", "Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Vasospasm peaks between days 4 and 14 after SAH. Nimodipine 60 mg 4-hourly for 21 days improves outcome. Keep the patient euvolaemic: fluid restriction is AVOIDED after SAH, because hyponatraemia is often cerebral salt wasting and hypovolaemia worsens ischaemia.', 'Neurology'),
(91, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A right-handed patient suddenly develops weakness of the right face and arm (more than the leg), right-sided sensory loss and expressive and receptive aphasia. The artery most likely blocked is the:', '["Left middle cerebral artery", "Basilar artery", "Left anterior cerebral artery", "Right middle cerebral artery"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Signs are on the opposite side to the lesion. Face and arm more than leg points to the MCA, and aphasia means the dominant (usually left) hemisphere. The anterior cerebral artery mainly affects the contralateral LEG; the basilar artery causes brainstem signs, coma or locked-in syndrome.', 'Neurology'),
(92, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient develops sudden vertigo, vomiting, difficulty swallowing and a hoarse voice. There is loss of pain and temperature sensation on the LEFT side of the face and the RIGHT side of the body, a left Horner''s syndrome and left-sided ataxia. The likely lesion is:', '["Left lateral medullary infarct (PICA / vertebral)", "Left middle cerebral artery territory infarct", "Right internal capsule lacunar infarct", "Basilar artery occlusion with locked-in state"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Wallenberg syndrome produces crossed sensory loss (face on the same side, body on the opposite side), same-side Horner''s, ataxia, dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus), and vertigo. There is usually no limb weakness. Check swallowing before giving anything by mouth.', 'Neurology'),
(93, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'After a brainstem stroke, a patient cannot move any limb or speak, but is fully awake and aware and can blink and move the eyes vertically on command. This is:', '["Brainstem death", "Akinetic mutism", "Persistent vegetative state", "Locked-in syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A ventral pontine lesion (basilar artery) cuts the motor pathways but spares consciousness and vertical eye movements, so the patient can communicate by blinking. A vegetative state has wakefulness WITHOUT awareness. Talk to these patients and explain care, as they hear and understand everything.', 'Neurology'),
(94, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient is still having a generalised seizure 10 minutes after two adequate IV doses of lorazepam. The recommended next step is:', '["A second-line IV drug, e.g. levetiracetam 60 mg/kg", "IV haloperidol to control the associated agitation", "Oral carbamazepine given down a nasogastric tube", "A third IV dose of lorazepam 4 mg straight away"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Established status epilepticus (after two benzodiazepine doses) needs a second-line IV agent: levetiracetam, fosphenytoin/phenytoin (with ECG monitoring) or valproate. If seizures continue (refractory status), give general anaesthesia (propofol, midazolam or thiopental) with intubation, ideally within 30–60 minutes of onset.', 'Neurology'),
(95, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A student has fever, headache, neck stiffness, a purpuric rash that does not blanch under a glass, and BP 85/50 mmHg. The priority action is:', '["Do a lumbar puncture first to identify the organism", "Wait for blood culture results to choose the antibiotic", "Do a CT head first, then give antibiotics if it is clear", "Give IV ceftriaxone immediately, before any LP or CT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is meningococcal septicaemia. Every hour of delay increases mortality. Take blood cultures if it causes no delay, but give antibiotics immediately; an LP is contraindicated with shock and a spreading rash. Use droplet precautions and give prophylaxis to close contacts.', 'Neurology'),
(96, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A farmer with 3 weeks of headache, fever and weight loss develops a sixth cranial nerve palsy. CSF shows raised pressure, lymphocytes, protein 2.5 g/L and a CSF:blood glucose ratio of 0.25. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Viral meningitis", "Tuberculous meningitis", "Subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Acute bacterial (pneumococcal) meningitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A subacute course over weeks, cranial nerve palsies, lymphocytic CSF, very high protein and very low glucose suggest TB meningitis (cryptococcal meningitis in HIV is the main alternative). Bacterial meningitis is acute with NEUTROPHILS; viral meningitis has lymphocytes but a NORMAL glucose and only mildly raised protein.', 'Neurology'),
(97, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A known alcoholic is admitted confused and unsteady on his feet, with nystagmus and weakness of eye movements. Before giving IV dextrose, the nurse should ensure he receives:', '["IV naloxone — this is opioid toxicity", "Oral lactulose — this is hepatic encephalopathy", "IV thiamine — this is Wernicke''s encephalopathy", "IV diazepam — this is delirium tremens"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The triad of confusion, ataxia and ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus is Wernicke''s encephalopathy (thiamine deficiency). Glucose without thiamine can make it worse. Untreated, it progresses to Korsakoff syndrome: permanent amnesia with confabulation.', 'Neurology'),
(98, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'Three days after admission for a fracture, a heavy drinker becomes agitated and disoriented, with visual hallucinations (''insects on the wall''), coarse tremor, sweating, fever, a pulse of 130 and high BP. The diagnosis is:', '["Korsakoff syndrome", "Delirium tremens", "Wernicke''s encephalopathy", "Hepatic encephalopathy"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Delirium tremens peaks 48–96 h after the last drink: delirium, visual hallucinations, tremor and autonomic overactivity (fever, tachycardia, hypertension, sweating). It can be fatal. Treat with benzodiazepines (e.g. chlordiazepoxide or IV diazepam), fluids, electrolytes and thiamine.', 'Neurology'),
(99, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient on an SSRI antidepressant was given tramadol. Within hours she is agitated and sweating, with a temperature of 39.5 °C, dilated pupils, diarrhoea, brisk reflexes and inducible clonus that is worse in the legs. The diagnosis is:', '["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Malignant hyperthermia", "Serotonin syndrome", "Anticholinergic toxicity"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Serotonin syndrome develops within hours of combining serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, tramadol, linezolid, MDMA). Key features are neuromuscular hyperactivity (clonus, hyperreflexia), autonomic instability and agitation. NMS develops over days with ''lead-pipe'' rigidity and SLOW reflexes; anticholinergic toxicity gives DRY skin and absent bowel sounds.', 'Neurology'),
(100, 'Identify the Disease: Neurological', 'A patient started on IV haloperidol 4 days ago now has a temperature of 40 °C, generalised ''lead-pipe'' rigidity, slowed movements, fluctuating BP, sweating and a creatine kinase of 25,000 U/L. The diagnosis is:', '["Malignant hyperthermia", "Serotonin syndrome", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Heat stroke"]'::jsonb, 2, 'NMS follows dopamine-blocking drugs and develops over days: hyperthermia, severe rigidity, altered consciousness, autonomic instability and a very high CK (risk of rhabdomyolysis and AKI). Stop the antipsychotic, cool and give fluids, and consider dantrolene or bromocriptine.', 'Neurology'),
(101, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient has K⁺ 7.2 mmol/L with peaked T waves. Which treatment actually REMOVES potassium from the body, rather than moving it into cells or protecting the heart?', '["Insulin with 50 mL of 50% dextrose", "IV 10% calcium gluconate", "Calcium resonium (a potassium binder)", "Nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Calcium gluconate stabilises the myocardium (no effect on the K⁺ level). Insulin–dextrose and salbutamol shift K⁺ into cells temporarily (it returns in hours). Only potassium binders, loop diuretics (if urine is passed) or dialysis remove K⁺ from the body.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(102, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'An ECG shows flattened T waves, ST depression, a prominent extra wave after the T wave, and an apparently long QT interval. The most likely electrolyte disturbance is:', '["Hypocalcaemia", "Hypokalaemia", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypermagnesaemia"]'::jsonb, 1, 'U waves, flat or inverted T waves and ST depression are classic for hypokalaemia; the T–U fusion looks like a long QT. Hypocalcaemia lengthens the ST segment (long QT) WITHOUT U waves. Hyperkalaemia gives peaked T waves.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(103, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'The day after a total thyroidectomy, a patient complains of tingling around the mouth and in the fingertips. Tapping in front of the ear makes the face twitch, and inflating a BP cuff causes carpal spasm. The most likely cause is:', '["Hypomagnesaemia from IV fluid dilution", "Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety", "Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury", "Thyroid storm from gland handling"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Chvostek''s and Trousseau''s signs with perioral paraesthesia after thyroid surgery = hypoparathyroidism with hypocalcaemia. Check calcium urgently and give IV 10% calcium gluconate if severe; keep it at the bedside after thyroid surgery. Check magnesium, as low Mg also prevents correction.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(104, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A woman with metastatic breast cancer has thirst, polyuria, constipation, abdominal pain, confusion and a short QT interval on the ECG. The most likely cause and first treatment are:', '["Hypercalcaemia — IV saline, then a bisphosphonate", "Hypokalaemia — IV potassium chloride replacement", "Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction", "Hypermagnesaemia — IV 10% calcium gluconate"]'::jsonb, 0, '''Bones, stones, abdominal groans and psychic moans'' with a short QT = hypercalcaemia. It causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus and dehydration, so aggressive saline rehydration comes first, followed by IV zoledronic acid or pamidronate.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(105, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient with a head injury has Na⁺ 124 mmol/L, serum osmolality 260 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 550 mOsm/kg and urine Na⁺ 60 mmol/L. BP, heart rate, CVP and skin turgor are normal and the weight is stable. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH)", "Cranial diabetes insipidus", "Primary (psychogenic) polydipsia", "Cerebral salt wasting syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Hypotonic hyponatraemia with inappropriately concentrated urine, high urine sodium and a EUVOLAEMIC patient = SIADH, treated with fluid restriction. Cerebral salt wasting has the same urine results but the patient is HYPOVOLAEMIC. DI causes HYPERnatraemia with dilute urine; polydipsia gives very dilute urine.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(106, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'After a subarachnoid haemorrhage, a patient has Na⁺ 125 mmol/L and high urine sodium. He is tachycardic, with a low CVP, a postural BP drop, weight loss and a negative fluid balance. The diagnosis and correct treatment are:', '["Cerebral salt wasting — give IV saline", "Adrenal failure — fludrocortisone only", "Cranial DI — give desmopressin", "SIADH — strict fluid restriction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Cerebral salt wasting is renal sodium loss with VOLUME DEPLETION. Fluid restriction (the SIADH treatment) would worsen hypovolaemia and vasospasm after SAH. Distinguishing the two depends on assessing volume status.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(107, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'After pituitary surgery, a patient passes 300 mL/h of urine with specific gravity 1.002. Serum Na⁺ is 152 mmol/L and the patient is very thirsty. The diagnosis and treatment are:', '["Cranial diabetes insipidus — desmopressin", "Hyperglycaemic osmotic diuresis — insulin", "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus — desmopressin", "SIADH with natriuresis — fluid restriction"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Lack of ADH after pituitary surgery or head injury causes large volumes of dilute urine and hypernatraemia; desmopressin corrects it. In nephrogenic DI (lithium, hypercalcaemia) the kidney does not respond to desmopressin. Hyperglycaemic osmotic diuresis gives a HIGH urine specific gravity.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(108, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient with chronic hyponatraemia (Na⁺ 108 mmol/L) is being treated. To avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome, sodium should rise by no more than:', '["15 mmol/L in the first 12 hours", "8–10 mmol/L in any 24 hours", "1 mmol/L per hour for the first 24 hours", "20 mmol/L in 24 hours"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia can cause central pontine myelinolysis (dysarthria, dysphagia, quadriparesis, locked-in state) days later. Limit the rise to 8–10 mmol/L in 24 h (8 or less in high-risk patients such as alcoholics and the malnourished). Even in symptomatic patients, raise Na⁺ by only 4–6 mmol/L quickly with hypertonic saline.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(109, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient''s blood results are Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 100, HCO₃⁻ 10 mmol/L, pH 7.15. The anion gap and the type of acidosis are:', '["Anion gap 50 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 10 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 30 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 30 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) = 140 − 110 = 30 (normal about 8–12). A raised gap means unmeasured acids: MUDPILES — methanol, uraemia, DKA, paracetamol/paraldehyde, iron/isoniazid, lactic acid, ethylene glycol, salicylates.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(110, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient with profuse cholera-like diarrhoea has pH 7.25, HCO₃⁻ 14, Na⁺ 138 and Cl⁻ 118 mmol/L. This is:', '["Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis from chloride loss in stool", "Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis", "High anion gap metabolic acidosis from lactate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Anion gap = 138 − (118 + 14) = 6, which is normal. Losing bicarbonate in stool, with chloride retained in its place, gives a hyperchloraemic acidosis. Other causes include renal tubular acidosis, large volumes of 0.9% saline, acetazolamide and ureteric diversions.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(111, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient who has been vomiting repeatedly for 5 days has pH 7.52, HCO₃⁻ 38 mmol/L, Cl⁻ 85 mmol/L and K⁺ 2.8 mmol/L. This is:', '["Primary respiratory alkalosis with hypokalaemia", "Hyperchloraemic hyperkalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Hyperchloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Vomiting loses HCl, raising bicarbonate. Volume depletion activates aldosterone, so the kidneys retain sodium and lose K⁺ and H⁺ (sometimes a paradoxically acid urine). Treat with 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride. Pyloric stenosis in infants gives the same picture.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(112, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient who took a large aspirin overdose has tinnitus, pH 7.45, PaCO₂ 25 mmHg and HCO₃⁻ 15 mmol/L. The acid–base disturbance is:', '["Respiratory alkalosis with full metabolic compensation", "Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic acidosis with full respiratory compensation", "Primary metabolic alkalosis with a compensating acidosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'For a metabolic acidosis with HCO₃⁻ 15, the expected PaCO₂ (Winter''s formula) = 1.5 × 15 + 8 = 30.5 ± 2. A PaCO₂ of 25 is lower than expected, so a separate respiratory alkalosis is present, from direct salicylate stimulation of the respiratory centre. Compensation never brings the pH fully to normal; a normal pH here means two processes.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(113, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A patient in DKA has pH 7.05, HCO₃⁻ 8 mmol/L and PaCO₂ 32 mmHg. Using Winter''s formula, this indicates:', '["Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation", "Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation", "Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory acidosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × 8 + 8 = 20 ± 2 mmHg. An actual PaCO₂ of 32 is much higher, so the patient is not blowing off enough CO₂ (exhaustion, reduced consciousness, chest problem). This is a danger sign and needs urgent senior review.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(114, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'After prolonged hypotension, a patient has oliguric AKI with urine Na⁺ 45 mmol/L, fractional excretion of sodium 3%, urine osmolality 300 mOsm/kg, and ''muddy brown'' granular casts on urine microscopy. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Pre-renal AKI", "Acute interstitial nephritis", "Post-renal (obstructive) AKI", "Acute tubular necrosis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Damaged tubules cannot conserve sodium or concentrate urine: FeNa above 2%, urine Na above 40, isosmotic urine and muddy brown casts. Pre-renal AKI shows avid sodium retention (FeNa below 1%, urine Na below 20, concentrated urine, bland sediment).', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(115, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'Ten days after starting a penicillin and an NSAID, a patient develops fever, a maculopapular rash, rising creatinine, blood eosinophilia and white cells (eosinophils) in the urine, with no casts of red cells. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Acute tubular necrosis", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Pre-renal AKI from dehydration", "Acute interstitial nephritis (drug-induced)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Drug-induced AIN (penicillins, NSAIDs, PPIs) is an allergic reaction: fever, rash, eosinophilia, sterile pyuria/eosinophiluria and AKI. Stop the culprit drug; steroids may help.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(116, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'An elderly man found on the floor after a 2-day ''long lie'' passes dark brown urine. Dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows almost no red cells. CK is 60,000 U/L and K⁺ 6.4 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction", "Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria", "Acute glomerulonephritis", "Ureteric stone"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The dipstick detects the haem in myoglobin as ''blood'', but few red cells are seen. A massive CK rise and hyperkalaemia from muscle breakdown confirm rhabdomyolysis. Give aggressive IV fluids (target urine output about 200–300 mL/h) and monitor K⁺ and kidney function. Haemolysis also gives a positive dipstick, but without a huge CK rise.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(117, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'Two weeks after a sore throat, a 9-year-old has cola-coloured urine, puffy eyelids, BP 150/100 mmHg, reduced urine output and red cell casts in the urine. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["IgA nephropathy (Berger''s disease)", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome", "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A latent period of 1–3 weeks after streptococcal pharyngitis (or 3–6 weeks after skin infection), then haematuria, hypertension, oedema, oliguria and red cell casts = nephritic syndrome. IgA nephropathy causes haematuria 1–2 DAYS after an infection; nephrotic syndrome is heavy proteinuria and low albumin WITHOUT haematuria.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(118, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'A 3-year-old has bloody diarrhoea, and a week later becomes pale and oliguric. Bloods show Hb 6 g/dL with fragmented red cells on the film, platelets 40 × 10⁹/L, and a NORMAL prothrombin time. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (e.g. E. coli O157)", "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"]'::jsonb, 1, 'HUS = microangiopathic haemolytic anaemia + thrombocytopenia + AKI, typically after Shiga-toxin E. coli diarrhoea in children. Clotting is NORMAL (unlike DIC). TTP usually affects adults, with fever and neurological features. Antibiotics are generally avoided in E. coli O157.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(119, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'During a first haemodialysis session for a urea of 60 mmol/L, a patient develops headache, nausea, restlessness, confusion and then a seizure. The most likely cause is:', '["Rebound hyperkalaemia after dialysis", "Venous air embolism from the circuit", "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome", "Hypoglycaemia during dialysis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'If urea is removed from the blood faster than from the brain, water moves into brain cells, causing cerebral oedema. Prevent it with short, low-efficiency first sessions (low blood flow and small filters) for very uraemic patients.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(120, 'Renal, Fluids, Electrolytes and Acid–Base', 'Which of the following is NOT an indication for URGENT dialysis in acute kidney injury?', '["Creatinine 400 µmol/L with normal K⁺ and pH and no symptoms", "Uraemic pericarditis with a pericardial friction rub", "K⁺ of 7.0 mmol/L that does not respond to medical treatment", "Pulmonary oedema not responding to high-dose diuretics"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Urgent indications (AEIOU): refractory Acidosis, refractory hyperkalaemia (Electrolytes), dialysable Intoxication (lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate), refractory fluid Overload, Uraemic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding). A raised creatinine alone is not an indication.', 'Renal & Fluid Balance'),
(121, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A 75-year-old with type 2 diabetes is drowsy and severely dehydrated. Glucose is 52 mmol/L, serum osmolality 360 mOsm/kg, pH 7.33, HCO₃⁻ 20 mmol/L and blood ketones 1.0 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:', '["Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS)", "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Euglycaemic DKA", "Metformin-associated lactic acidosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'HHS: marked hyperglycaemia (usually above 30), osmolality above 320, profound dehydration and NO significant ketoacidosis (pH above 7.3, HCO₃⁻ above 15, ketones below 3). Rehydrate slowly with 0.9% saline; give insulin at a low rate (0.05 units/kg/h) only once glucose stops falling with fluids alone; give VTE prophylaxis.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(122, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient on empagliflozin (an SGLT2 inhibitor) is admitted with vomiting after surgery. Glucose is 11 mmol/L, pH 7.10, HCO₃⁻ 9 mmol/L and blood ketones 5.2 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:', '["Alcoholic ketoacidosis", "Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis", "Lactic acidosis", "Starvation ketosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'SGLT2 inhibitors lower glucose through the urine, so DKA can occur with a near-normal glucose. Severe acidosis (pH 7.10) with ketones above 3 is DKA, not simple starvation ketosis (which rarely drops pH below 7.3). Treat as DKA, giving dextrose alongside insulin. These drugs are stopped before surgery.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(123, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient in DKA has an initial K⁺ of 3.0 mmol/L. The correct management is:', '["Start the fixed-rate insulin at 0.1 units/kg/h straight away", "Give IV sodium bicarbonate first to correct the acidosis quickly", "Give fluids with potassium, and delay insulin until K⁺ is above 3.3", "Give a 10-unit IV insulin bolus, then start the insulin infusion"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Insulin drives potassium into cells. Starting it with a low K⁺ can cause fatal hypokalaemia and arrhythmias. Replace potassium first. Bicarbonate is not routinely used, and boluses are not recommended.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(124, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with poorly controlled hyperthyroidism develops fever of 40.5 °C, AF at 160/min, agitation, diarrhoea and heart failure after an emergency appendicectomy. When should iodine (Lugol''s iodine) be given?', '["Never — iodine is contraindicated in thyroid storm", "At least 1 hour AFTER the antithyroid drug (propylthiouracil or carbimazole)", "At the same time as the antithyroid drug", "Before the antithyroid drug, to block hormone release quickly"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In thyroid storm, give beta-blockade (propranolol), a thionamide (PTU, which also blocks T4→T3 conversion), then iodine at least an hour later, plus hydrocortisone and cooling. Iodine given before the thionamide provides substrate for MORE hormone synthesis.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(125, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'An elderly woman with untreated hypothyroidism is found hypothermic (32.5 °C), bradycardic and drowsy, with hypoventilation, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia and puffy non-pitting oedema. Before IV thyroid hormone is given, the patient should receive:', '["Strict fluid restriction to 500 mL/day", "An IV insulin infusion", "Rapid active external rewarming with a heating blanket", "IV hydrocortisone"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is myxoedema coma. Adrenal insufficiency may coexist, and thyroid hormone speeds cortisol breakdown, so give hydrocortisone first to avoid precipitating an adrenal crisis. Rewarm passively and slowly (rapid rewarming causes vasodilation and collapse), and support ventilation and glucose.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(126, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient who stopped long-term prednisolone suddenly 2 weeks ago presents with vomiting, abdominal pain and hypotension unresponsive to 2 L of fluid. Na⁺ is 125, K⁺ 6.0 and glucose 2.8 mmol/L. The diagnosis and immediate treatment are:', '["SIADH — strict fluid restriction to 800 mL/day", "Myxoedema crisis — IV levothyroxine straight away", "Septic shock — antibiotics and noradrenaline only", "Adrenal crisis — IV hydrocortisone 100 mg now"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Abrupt withdrawal of long-term steroids suppresses the adrenal axis. Hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia and refractory hypotension = adrenal crisis. Do not delay hydrocortisone for tests. SIADH does not cause hyperkalaemia or shock.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(127, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A 40-year-old has episodes of pounding headache, sweating and palpitations with BP up to 240/130 mmHg. A tumour is found in the adrenal gland. Before surgery, the correct drug order is:', '["Alpha-blocker first, then add a beta-blocker", "Beta-blocker first, then add an alpha-blocker", "An ACE inhibitor alone at the maximum dose", "A loop diuretic to reduce circulating volume"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In phaeochromocytoma, a beta-blocker alone leaves alpha (vasoconstrictor) effects unopposed, causing a hypertensive crisis. Alpha-block first (10–14 days, with salt and fluid loading), then add a beta-blocker for tachycardia.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(128, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A man with liver cirrhosis, ascites and splenomegaly vomits large amounts of fresh blood. Apart from resuscitation and urgent endoscopy with band ligation, which drug therapy has been shown to reduce mortality?', '["Terlipressin and prophylactic IV antibiotics", "A PPI infusion and IV tranexamic acid", "Routine fresh frozen plasma and vitamin K", "IV propranolol during the bleed"]'::jsonb, 0, 'In variceal bleeding, a vasoactive drug (terlipressin) lowers portal pressure, and prophylactic antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone) reduce infection, rebleeding and death. Tranexamic acid did not help in GI bleeding (HALT-IT trial). Restrictive transfusion (Hb about 7–8 g/dL) avoids raising portal pressure. Beta-blockers are for prevention, not the acute bleed.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(129, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'After a night of heavy drinking and forceful vomiting, a man has severe retrosternal chest pain. There is a crackling feeling under the skin of the neck, a ''crunching'' sound with each heartbeat, and a left pleural effusion. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Perforated duodenal ulcer", "Acute inferior myocardial infarction", "Mallory–Weiss tear (mucosal tear)", "Boerhaave syndrome (oesophageal rupture)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Spontaneous oesophageal rupture after forceful vomiting causes chest pain, surgical emphysema and pneumomediastinum (Hamman''s crunch) and a left effusion, with rapid progression to mediastinitis and sepsis. Keep nil by mouth, give broad-spectrum antibiotics and refer to surgery urgently. A Mallory–Weiss tear is a mucosal tear causing haematemesis, without emphysema.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(130, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient taking regular NSAIDs has sudden severe epigastric pain that becomes generalised. The abdomen is rigid and ''board-like'', he lies completely still, and an erect chest X-ray shows air under the diaphragm. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute cholecystitis", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Acute pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sudden pain with generalised peritonitis (board-like rigidity, patient lying still) and pneumoperitoneum = perforated viscus, most often a peptic ulcer. Keep nil by mouth, insert an NG tube, give IV fluids, PPI and antibiotics, and refer for surgery.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(131, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A heavy drinker has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, relieved by leaning forward, with vomiting. There is bruising in the flanks and around the umbilicus, and serum lipase is 8 times normal. The diagnosis is:', '["Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Epigastric pain to the back with lipase above 3 times normal = acute pancreatitis. Grey Turner''s (flank) and Cullen''s (periumbilical) signs indicate retroperitoneal haemorrhage and severe disease. Give goal-directed fluids, analgesia, early enteral feeding and organ support; watch for ARDS, AKI and hypocalcaemia.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(132, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A 75-year-old with atrial fibrillation (not anticoagulated) has sudden, severe abdominal pain that seems out of proportion to the examination — the abdomen is soft with little tenderness. Lactate is 6 mmol/L, and bloody diarrhoea appears later. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Perforated peptic ulcer", "Acute haemorrhagic pancreatitis", "Perforated sigmoid diverticulitis", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia"]'::jsonb, 3, 'An embolus from AF blocks the SMA. Pain far worse than the signs, rising lactate and later bloody stools and peritonitis (once the bowel infarcts) are typical. Arrange urgent CT angiography and surgical or radiological revascularisation; mortality is very high.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(133, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with gallstones has fever with rigors, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain, and is now confused with BP 80/50 mmHg. The diagnosis and definitive treatment are:', '["Acute viral hepatitis — supportive care", "Acute pancreatitis — fluids only", "Acute cholecystitis — antibiotics and elective cholecystectomy in 6 weeks", "Acute suppurative (ascending) cholangitis — antibiotics plus urgent biliary drainage (ERCP)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Charcot''s triad (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain) plus hypotension and confusion = Reynolds'' pentad, which means suppurative cholangitis with an obstructed, infected bile duct. Antibiotics alone are not enough; the duct must be decompressed urgently. Cholecystitis usually causes no jaundice.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(134, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with cirrhosis who had a GI bleed yesterday is now confused and drowsy, with a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands and a sweet musty breath odour. The correct management includes:', '["A protein-free diet for one week to stop ammonia production", "High-dose diuretics to clear the ascites and improve the confusion", "A benzodiazepine to settle the agitation and allow nursing care", "Lactulose titrated to 2–3 soft stools a day, and treat the cause"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hepatic encephalopathy is precipitated by GI bleeding (a protein load), infection, constipation, sedatives, hypokalaemia and dehydration. Lactulose reduces ammonia absorption. Protein restriction worsens malnutrition and is no longer advised. Benzodiazepines and over-diuresis worsen encephalopathy.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(135, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with cirrhosis and ascites develops fever, mild abdominal tenderness and worsening confusion. A diagnostic paracentesis shows ascitic neutrophils 400 cells/mm³ with a single organism. The diagnosis and treatment are:', '["Secondary peritonitis from bowel perforation — emergency laparotomy", "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis — IV cefotaxime (or similar) plus IV albumin", "Tuberculous peritonitis — anti-TB therapy", "Hepatorenal syndrome — terlipressin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ascitic neutrophils of 250/mm³ or more, without a surgical source, = SBP (usually one organism, e.g. E. coli). IV albumin on days 1 and 3 reduces hepatorenal syndrome and death. Secondary peritonitis usually shows multiple organisms, very high protein and low glucose.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(136, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with cirrhosis and tense ascites has creatinine rising over 3 days, urine output below 400 mL/day and urine Na⁺ below 10 mmol/L. There is no shock, no nephrotoxic drug use, normal kidneys on ultrasound, and no improvement after 2 days of stopping diuretics and giving IV albumin. The diagnosis is:', '["Obstructive (post-renal) AKI", "Acute tubular necrosis", "Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS-AKI)", "Pre-renal AKI from diuretics"]'::jsonb, 2, 'HRS is functional kidney failure caused by severe vasodilation in the gut circulation, with intense renal vasoconstriction (avid sodium retention). It is diagnosed when there is no response to stopping diuretics plus albumin volume expansion, with no shock, nephrotoxins or structural disease. Treat with terlipressin plus albumin; liver transplant is definitive.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(137, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A severely malnourished patient starts full nasogastric feeding. On day 3 she becomes confused and weak, with breathlessness, oedema and arrhythmias. Phosphate is 0.3 mmol/L, with low potassium and magnesium. The diagnosis is:', '["Refeeding syndrome", "Dumping syndrome", "Wernicke''s encephalopathy", "Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Feeding triggers insulin release, driving phosphate, K⁺ and Mg²⁺ into cells. Severe hypophosphataemia causes muscle weakness (including the diaphragm), heart failure, arrhythmias and confusion. Prevent it by starting feeds slowly (e.g. 10 kcal/kg/day in high-risk patients), giving thiamine, and monitoring and replacing electrolytes.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(138, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'After massive fluid resuscitation for trauma, a ventilated patient has a tense, distended abdomen, rising peak airway pressures and oliguria. Bladder pressure is 25 mmHg. The diagnosis is:', '["Paralytic ileus with normal abdominal pressure", "Intra-abdominal hypertension grade I", "Acute urinary retention", "Abdominal compartment syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Normal intra-abdominal pressure is about 5–7 mmHg; IAH grade I is 12–15, II 16–20, III 21–25, IV above 25. Abdominal compartment syndrome = sustained IAP above 20 mmHg WITH new organ dysfunction (renal, respiratory, cardiac). It may need decompressive laparotomy. IAP is measured through the bladder, zeroed at the iliac crest in the mid-axillary line.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(139, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient with known ulcerative colitis has fever, a pulse of 120, a distended tender abdomen, reduced bowel sounds and a transverse colon diameter of 8 cm on X-ray. The diagnosis is:', '["Small bowel obstruction", "Toxic megacolon", "Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie''s syndrome)", "Sigmoid volvulus"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Toxic megacolon is colonic dilatation (above 6 cm) with systemic toxicity in severe colitis (UC, C. difficile). Perforation risk is high. Stop anticholinergics and opioids, give IV steroids and fluids, and arrange early surgical review. Volvulus shows a ''coffee bean'' loop; Ogilvie''s syndrome has no colitis or toxicity.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(140, 'Endocrine, Gastrointestinal and Liver Emergencies', 'A patient who took an overdose of gliclazide (a sulfonylurea) keeps becoming hypoglycaemic despite repeated IV dextrose boluses. The most appropriate additional treatment is:', '["IV hydrocortisone", "Octreotide", "IV flumazenil", "IV glucagon infusion as the long-term solution"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Sulfonylureas make the pancreas release insulin; each dextrose bolus triggers more insulin and rebound hypoglycaemia. Octreotide inhibits insulin secretion. Glucagon helps only briefly (and needs liver glycogen); continuous dextrose infusion and close monitoring for 24 h or more are also needed.', 'GI, Endocrine & Metabolic'),
(141, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'According to Sepsis-3, septic shock is identified clinically by:', '["Systolic BP below 90 mmHg with a qSOFA score of 2 or more after fluids", "A lactate above 2 mmol/L on its own, whatever the blood pressure", "Vasopressors needed for MAP ≥ 65 AND lactate > 2 mmol/L after adequate fluids", "MAP below 65 mmHg with a lactate above 4 mmol/L, before any fluids are given"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sepsis-3 (2016): sepsis = life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection; septic shock = sepsis needing vasopressors for MAP ≥65 with lactate above 2 mmol/L after adequate fluids. qSOFA is a bedside screening tool, not a definition.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(142, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'Under Sepsis-3, organ dysfunction in a patient with infection is identified by an acute rise in the SOFA score of:', '["3 points or more", "2 or more SIRS criteria", "2 points or more", "1 point or more on qSOFA"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A rise of 2 or more SOFA points reflects organ dysfunction, with an in-hospital mortality above 10%. SIRS criteria were dropped from the definition as non-specific; qSOFA (RR ≥22, altered mentation, SBP ≤100) is a screening prompt, needing 2 of 3.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(143, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A febrile patient with pneumonia has BP 80/40 mmHg, a bounding pulse, warm flushed peripheries, a wide pulse pressure and rapid capillary refill. The haemodynamic pattern is most likely:', '["Distributive shock — low SVR with a normal or high cardiac output", "Hypovolaemic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR", "Obstructive shock — low cardiac output with high CVP", "Cardiogenic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Early (''warm'') septic shock is distributive: inflammatory vasodilation lowers SVR, causing warm peripheries and a wide pulse pressure, and cardiac output is often raised. Give fluids, then noradrenaline. Later sepsis can show myocardial depression (''cold'' shock).', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(144, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'After a road traffic collision, a man has HR 132, BP 85/50 mmHg, RR 34, is anxious and confused, and passes 10 mL/h of urine. By ATLS, the estimated blood loss class is:', '["Class III (30–40% of blood volume)", "Class II (15–30%)", "Class IV (more than 40%)", "Class I (less than 15%)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Class III: HR 120–140, systolic BP REDUCED, RR 30–40, urine 5–15 mL/h, anxious or confused. Class II has a normal systolic BP with a narrowed pulse pressure; Class IV has HR above 140, a very low BP, lethargy and negligible urine output. Class III usually needs blood transfusion.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(145, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A trauma patient with major haemorrhage arrives 1 hour after injury. Tranexamic acid should be given as:', '["2 g as a single IV bolus — within 6 hours of injury", "1 g over 10 min — only if fibrinogen is below 1 g/L", "1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — after 3 hours only", "1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — within 3 h of injury"]'::jsonb, 3, 'CRASH-2 showed tranexamic acid reduces bleeding deaths when given within 3 hours; started after 3 hours it increased deaths from bleeding. Give it as early as possible. (The WOMAN trial applies the same 3-hour principle to postpartum haemorrhage.)', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(146, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'The ''lethal triad'' that worsens bleeding and death after major trauma is:', '["Hypotension, hypoxia and hypercapnia", "Tachycardia, hypotension and oliguria", "Hypothermia, hyperkalaemia and hypocalcaemia", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hypothermia impairs clotting enzymes and platelets; acidosis reduces clotting factor activity; coagulopathy increases bleeding, which worsens shock and acidosis — a vicious cycle. Damage-control resuscitation means warming, balanced blood products (1:1:1), permissive hypotension and early haemorrhage control. Calcium is also monitored.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(147, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A patient with septic shock oozes from cannula sites and the gums and has new petechiae. Platelets are 35 × 10⁹/L, PT and APTT are prolonged, fibrinogen is 0.8 g/L and D-dimer is very high. The diagnosis is:', '["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Vitamin K deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'DIC consumes platelets AND clotting factors (low platelets, prolonged PT/APTT, low fibrinogen) with excess fibrinolysis (high D-dimer). TTP and ITP have NORMAL clotting tests; vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT but leaves platelets and fibrinogen normal. Treat the cause and replace products if bleeding.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(148, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'On day 7 of a heparin infusion, a patient''s platelets fall from 250 to 90 × 10⁹/L and a new DVT develops. The most likely diagnosis and correct action are:', '["ITP — give IV immunoglobulin and steroids, continue heparin", "DIC — give platelets and fresh frozen plasma, continue heparin", "HIT — stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant", "Dilutional thrombocytopenia — transfuse platelets, continue heparin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'HIT typically starts 5–10 days after heparin begins, with a platelet fall of more than 50% and paradoxical THROMBOSIS, caused by antibodies against heparin–PF4 complexes. Stopping heparin alone is not enough; an alternative anticoagulant is needed. Platelet transfusion is avoided. Do not start warfarin until platelets recover.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(149, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'Two hours into a blood transfusion, a patient develops severe breathlessness, fever, hypotension and bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray. JVP is not raised, BNP is normal, and furosemide does not help. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)", "Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction", "Anaphylactic transfusion reaction", "Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'TRALI is non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema within 6 h of transfusion (donor antibodies against recipient white cells), often with fever and HYPOtension. TACO is hydrostatic overload with HYPERtension, a raised JVP and BNP, and it responds to diuretics. Stop the transfusion and give respiratory support.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(150, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'Ten minutes into a blood transfusion, a patient develops fever, rigors, loin pain, hypotension and dark red urine, and starts oozing from the cannula site. The FIRST action is to:', '["Give IV chlorphenamine and continue the transfusion at the same rate", "Flush the rest of the unit in quickly with saline, then send samples", "Stop the transfusion and keep the vein open with saline via a new set", "Slow the transfusion and give paracetamol for the fever and rigors"]'::jsonb, 2, 'This is an acute haemolytic (ABO-incompatible) reaction. Stop the transfusion at once, maintain access with a new set (do not flush the remaining blood in), recheck patient ID against the unit, call the doctor and blood bank, return the unit, and send samples. Support BP and renal output; watch for DIC.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(151, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A 70 kg man has 40% total body surface area partial- and full-thickness burns and arrives 2 hours after the burn. Using the Parkland formula, the IV fluid rate for the next 6 hours should be about:', '["1,400 mL/h", "467 mL/h", "933 mL/h", "700 mL/h"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Parkland: 4 mL × 70 kg × 40% = 11,200 mL over 24 h FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN; half (5,600 mL) is given in the first 8 hours. Two hours have passed, so 5,600 mL must go in over the remaining 6 hours: 5,600 ÷ 6 ≈ 933 mL/h. Then titrate to urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/h.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(152, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A man was rescued from a burning house after 20 minutes in a smoke-filled room. Which finding is the strongest indication for EARLY intubation?', '["A carboxyhaemoglobin level of 8%", "SpO₂ of 97% on oxygen", "Superficial facial burns alone", "A hoarse voice with inspiratory stridor"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Hoarseness and stridor mean upper-airway swelling, which worsens rapidly over hours as fluid is given; delaying intubation can make it impossible later. Facial burns, singed nasal hair and soot alone call for close observation. SpO₂ is unreliable with carbon monoxide present.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(153, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A family is found drowsy at home with a generator running indoors. They have headache, nausea and confusion. SpO₂ reads 98%, and carboxyhaemoglobin is 28%. The most appropriate treatment is:', '["100% oxygen via a tight non-rebreather mask", "Oxygen titrated to an SpO₂ of 94–98%", "IV hydroxocobalamin 5 g over 15 minutes", "IV methylene blue 1–2 mg/kg over 5 minutes"]'::jsonb, 0, 'CO binds haemoglobin about 240 times more strongly than oxygen. 100% oxygen shortens the CO half-life from about 5 hours to about 1 hour; hyperbaric oxygen shortens it further (consider for loss of consciousness, pregnancy, neurological signs or very high COHb). SpO₂ is falsely normal, so do not titrate to it.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(154, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A patient rescued from a fire involving burning foam mattresses and plastics is comatose and hypotensive, with lactate 14 mmol/L and a central venous oxygen saturation of 90% (very high). The most likely additional poisoning and antidote are:', '["Cyanide — IV hydroxocobalamin", "Carbon monoxide — hyperbaric oxygen", "Methaemoglobinaemia — methylene blue", "Organophosphate — atropine"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Burning plastics, wool and foam release hydrogen cyanide, which blocks cytochrome oxidase. Cells cannot use oxygen, so lactate is very high and venous blood stays highly oxygenated (''arterialised''). Hydroxocobalamin (5 g IV) binds cyanide to form cyanocobalamin.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(155, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A farmer who drank a pesticide has pinpoint pupils, drooling, wheezing with copious bronchial secretions, vomiting, diarrhoea, HR 45 and muscle twitching. Atropine is being given in doubling doses. The best endpoint for adequate atropinisation is:', '["Complete disappearance of all muscle fasciculations", "A clear chest, with HR above 80 and systolic BP above 80", "Fully dilated pupils that no longer react to light", "A heart rate above 120/min with a dry, flushed skin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In organophosphate poisoning, death is mainly from bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm and respiratory failure. Titrate atropine until the chest is clear, HR is above 80 and systolic BP above 80, then maintain with an infusion. Pupil size is unreliable, and atropine does not reverse nicotinic effects (weakness, fasciculations) — pralidoxime and ventilation may be needed.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(156, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A patient who took staggered paracetamol overdoses over 2 days has ALT 4,000 IU/L, INR 3.2 and arterial pH 7.25 after full fluid resuscitation. The correct next step is:', '["Give activated charcoal and repeat the paracetamol level in 4 hours", "Give vitamin K to correct the INR, then plan discharge in 48 hours", "Refer urgently to a liver transplant centre (pH < 7.30 after fluids)", "Stop N-acetylcysteine, because more than 24 hours have now passed"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Continue NAC (it helps even late in established liver injury). King''s College criteria for paracetamol-induced liver failure: arterial pH below 7.30 after resuscitation, OR all of INR above 6.5, creatinine above 300 µmol/L and grade 3–4 encephalopathy. Do not correct the INR unless bleeding, as it is a prognostic marker.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(157, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'After a tricyclic antidepressant overdose, a patient is drowsy with dilated pupils, dry flushed skin, urinary retention, HR 130 and a QRS duration of 140 ms, and then has a seizure. The first-line treatment for the cardiotoxicity is:', '["IV sodium bicarbonate 8.4%", "IV flumazenil", "IV physostigmine", "IV amiodarone"]'::jsonb, 0, 'TCAs block cardiac sodium channels (wide QRS, ventricular arrhythmias) and cause anticholinergic and sedative effects. Sodium bicarbonate (an alkalinising sodium load) narrows the QRS and protects against arrhythmias. Flumazenil and physostigmine can provoke seizures and asystole; class I and III antiarrhythmics worsen toxicity.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(158, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'A farmer in northern Nigeria was bitten on the foot by a snake 12 hours ago. He now has bleeding gums, blood in the urine and oozing from a cannula site; blood in a dry glass tube does not clot after 20 minutes. The most likely snake and treatment are:', '["Dog bite with rabies — rabies vaccine", "Cobra envenoming with neurotoxic paralysis — neostigmine alone", "Scorpion sting — analgesia only", "Carpet viper (Echis ocellatus) envenoming — specific antivenom"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Echis ocellatus is the leading cause of snakebite deaths in West Africa. Its venom causes a consumptive coagulopathy, so blood fails to clot in the 20-minute whole blood clotting test (20WBCT). Give the appropriate polyvalent or Echis antivenom and repeat the 20WBCT every 6 hours. Cobras cause neurotoxicity (ptosis, paralysis) rather than bleeding.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(159, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'During general anaesthesia with sevoflurane and suxamethonium, a patient develops masseter rigidity, a rapidly rising end-tidal CO₂ despite increased ventilation, tachycardia, rising temperature and hyperkalaemia. The diagnosis and specific treatment are:', '["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — bromocriptine", "Thyroid storm — propylthiouracil and iodine", "Malignant hyperthermia — IV dantrolene", "Serotonin syndrome — cyproheptadine"]'::jsonb, 2, 'MH is an inherited ryanodine-receptor disorder triggered by volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium, causing a hypermetabolic crisis. A rising EtCO₂ is the earliest sign; hyperthermia is often late. Stop the triggers, hyperventilate with 100% O₂ on a clean circuit, give dantrolene, cool, and treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(160, 'Multi-Organ Failure, Shock, Trauma, Burns and Poisoning', 'Six hours after a tibial fracture is placed in a cast, a patient has severe pain not relieved by morphine, pain on passive stretching of the toes, and a tense swollen calf. Foot pulses are still palpable. The most likely diagnosis and action are:', '["Cellulitis under the cast — start IV flucloxacillin and elevate the leg", "Deep vein thrombosis — elevate the limb and start therapeutic heparin", "Arterial occlusion — excluded, because the foot pulses are present", "Compartment syndrome — split the cast and arrange urgent fasciotomy"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pain out of proportion and pain on passive stretch are the earliest signs. Pulses usually remain until very late, so their presence does NOT exclude compartment syndrome. Do not elevate the limb above the heart (this reduces perfusion). Fasciotomy is needed when compartment pressure is within 30 mmHg of diastolic BP.', 'Shock & Haemorrhage'),
(161, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A woman at 34 weeks has right upper quadrant pain, nausea and BP 150/100 mmHg. Bloods show haemolysis on the film, ALT 400 IU/L, platelets 60 × 10⁹/L, a normal glucose and normal clotting times. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["HELLP syndrome", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Acute fatty liver of pregnancy", "Obstetric cholestasis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'HELLP = Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets, usually with pre-eclampsia; RUQ pain reflects liver swelling (rupture risk). Acute fatty liver of pregnancy causes HYPOGLYCAEMIA, coagulopathy, high ammonia and liver failure. Cholestasis causes itching and raised bile acids. Treatment is magnesium, BP control and delivery.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(162, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'At 36 weeks a woman has 1 week of nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain, then becomes jaundiced and confused. Glucose is 2.1 mmol/L, INR 2.5, ammonia and uric acid are raised, and BP is only mildly elevated. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Acute fatty liver of pregnancy", "Acute viral hepatitis", "Hyperemesis gravidarum", "HELLP syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'AFLP is microvesicular fatty infiltration of the liver in the third trimester, causing true liver failure: hypoglycaemia, coagulopathy, raised ammonia, encephalopathy and often AKI. Treatment is prompt delivery and supportive ICU care (glucose, clotting products).', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(163, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is on a magnesium sulphate infusion. Which finding means the nurse should stop the infusion and call the doctor FIRST?', '["Serum magnesium of 2.5 mmol/L", "Urine output of 35 mL/h", "Absent patellar (knee-jerk) reflexes", "Respiratory rate of 14/min"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Loss of the patellar reflexes is the first sign of magnesium toxicity (about 3.5–5 mmol/L), before respiratory depression (5–6.5) and cardiac arrest (above 7.5). The therapeutic range is about 2–4 mmol/L, so 2.5 is safe; urine output of at least 25–30 mL/h and RR above 12 are acceptable. The antidote is 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(164, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'During labour, shortly after artificial rupture of membranes, a woman suddenly becomes breathless and cyanosed, collapses with severe hypotension and then has a cardiac arrest. Those who survive the first hour develop severe bleeding from DIC. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Amniotic fluid embolism", "Placental abruption", "Eclampsia", "Massive pulmonary thromboembolism"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Amniotic fluid embolism is sudden cardiorespiratory collapse during labour or delivery (or soon after), followed by early, severe DIC. Pulmonary embolism does not usually cause immediate DIC; eclampsia presents with seizures and hypertension; abruption starts with pain and bleeding.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(165, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'At 33 weeks a woman with pre-eclampsia has sudden constant abdominal pain and slight dark vaginal bleeding. The uterus is hard, ''woody'' and tender, the fetal heart rate is 90/min, and she is in shock out of proportion to the visible blood loss. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Ruptured vasa praevia", "Uterine rupture", "Major placenta praevia", "Concealed placental abruption"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Abruption (risk factors: hypertension, trauma, cocaine, smoking) causes painful bleeding, a tense tender uterus, fetal distress, and shock greater than the visible loss because blood is concealed behind the placenta. DIC is common. Placenta praevia is PAINLESS bleeding with a soft, non-tender uterus.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(166, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A woman attempting a vaginal birth after a previous caesarean section suddenly has severe abdominal pain. Contractions stop, the fetal heart rate drops to 70/min, the presenting part moves back up, and fetal parts are easily felt through the abdomen. The diagnosis is:', '["Placenta praevia", "Uterine rupture", "Placental abruption", "Normal second stage of labour"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Scar rupture: sudden pain, loss of contractions, loss of station of the presenting part, easily palpable fetal parts, fetal bradycardia and maternal shock. This needs an immediate laparotomy.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(167, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage from uterine atony. Which uterotonic is contraindicated because she has pre-eclampsia?', '["Misoprostol", "Oxytocin", "Ergometrine", "Carboprost"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ergometrine causes vasoconstriction and can trigger severe hypertension, stroke or eclampsia, so it is avoided in hypertension, pre-eclampsia and cardiac disease. Carboprost (prostaglandin F2α) is avoided in ASTHMA because it causes bronchospasm. Oxytocin and misoprostol are acceptable.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(168, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'To reduce deaths from postpartum haemorrhage, tranexamic acid 1 g IV should be given within:', '["12 hours of birth", "6 hours of birth", "3 hours of birth", "24 hours of birth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The WOMAN trial showed tranexamic acid reduces deaths from bleeding in PPH when given within 3 hours of birth; benefit falls with every 15 minutes of delay, and there is no benefit after 3 hours. A second 1 g dose can be given if bleeding continues after 30 minutes or restarts within 24 hours.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(169, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'Five weeks after a normal delivery, a previously healthy woman has worsening breathlessness, orthopnoea, swollen ankles and a resting heart rate of 120. Echo shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%, and she has no history of heart disease. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Postpartum anaemia only", "Peripartum cardiomyopathy", "Postpartum pulmonary embolism", "Pre-existing rheumatic mitral stenosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure with LV systolic dysfunction (EF below 45%) in late pregnancy or the months after delivery, with no other cause. It is more common in African women, multiparous women and those with pre-eclampsia. Treat as heart failure (avoiding ACE inhibitors before delivery); consider bromocriptine and anticoagulation.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(170, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A woman at 30 weeks of pregnancy has a cardiac arrest on the ward. CPR with manual left uterine displacement has started. If there is no return of spontaneous circulation, resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem caesarean section) should be started:', '["After 15 minutes of unsuccessful CPR", "After 30 minutes of CPR and senior review", "After 10 minutes of unsuccessful CPR", "By 4 minutes, to deliver the baby by 5 minutes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'From about 20 weeks, the uterus compresses the vena cava and aorta and makes CPR ineffective. Emptying the uterus improves venous return and maternal survival. The 4–5 minute rule applies wherever the arrest happens; do not move the patient to theatre.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(171, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A baby born at 28 weeks to a mother who did not receive antenatal steroids develops grunting, nasal flaring, chest recession and cyanosis within an hour of birth. Chest X-ray shows a diffuse ''ground-glass'' appearance with air bronchograms. The diagnosis is:', '["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn", "Meconium aspiration syndrome", "Respiratory distress syndrome", "Congenital diaphragmatic hernia"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Preterm surfactant deficiency causes alveolar collapse, giving a ground-glass X-ray with air bronchograms and worsening distress over the first hours. Treat with CPAP and endotracheal surfactant. Antenatal corticosteroids reduce the risk.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(172, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A term baby born by elective caesarean section (no labour) is breathing at 80/min with mild recession and needs little oxygen. Chest X-ray shows fluid in the horizontal fissure and prominent vascular markings. Symptoms settle over 48 hours. The diagnosis is:', '["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn", "Respiratory distress syndrome", "Group B streptococcal pneumonia", "Meconium aspiration syndrome"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Without labour, catecholamine-driven clearance of fetal lung fluid is delayed, giving TTN: tachypnoea with mild oxygen need that resolves within 24–72 hours. Always consider sepsis (GBS) if the baby is unwell or the course is atypical.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(173, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A baby is visibly jaundiced at 18 hours of age, with a falling haemoglobin. The mother is blood group O Rh-positive and the baby is A Rh-positive. The most likely cause is:', '["ABO haemolytic disease", "Breast-milk jaundice", "Biliary atresia", "Physiological jaundice"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Jaundice within 24 hours is ALWAYS pathological, usually haemolysis. Group O mothers have IgG anti-A/anti-B antibodies that cross the placenta and haemolyse A or B fetal red cells. Physiological jaundice starts after 24 h; breast-milk jaundice appears after the first week; biliary atresia gives conjugated jaundice with pale stools after 2 weeks.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(174, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 3-week-old baby who had been feeding well suddenly starts vomiting bright GREEN (bilious) fluid and becomes irritable, with a slightly distended abdomen. The most important diagnosis to exclude urgently is:', '["Malrotation with midgut volvulus", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux", "Overfeeding", "Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bilious vomiting in a newborn means obstruction below the ampulla and is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise; volvulus can infarct the whole midgut within hours. Pyloric stenosis causes NON-bilious projectile vomiting at 2–8 weeks, with an olive-shaped mass and hypochloraemic alkalosis.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(175, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 9-month-old has episodes of screaming with knees drawn up and pallor every 15–20 minutes, sleepy between attacks. He then passes stool mixed with blood and mucus (''redcurrant jelly''), and a sausage-shaped mass is felt in the right upper abdomen. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute gastroenteritis", "Infantile colic", "Intussusception", "Meckel''s diverticulum"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Intussusception (most common at 6–18 months) causes paroxysmal colicky pain with pallor, lethargy, a sausage-shaped mass and late redcurrant-jelly stool. Diagnose with ultrasound (target sign); treat with air-enema reduction if there is no peritonitis, otherwise surgery.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(176, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 3-year-old was diagnosed with croup 2 days ago but is now much worse, with high fever, a toxic appearance, copious thick purulent secretions from the trachea and stridor that does not improve with nebulised adrenaline. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Viral croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)", "Bacterial tracheitis", "Inhaled foreign body", "Acute epiglottitis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Bacterial tracheitis (often Staph aureus) usually follows a viral croup: the child becomes toxic and febrile, with thick purulent tracheal secretions and a poor response to croup treatments. It often needs intubation and IV antibiotics. Epiglottitis starts suddenly with drooling and NO barking cough.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(177, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 10 kg, 8-month-old infant has severe dehydration from diarrhoea (lethargic, sunken eyes, drinks poorly, very slow skin pinch). By WHO Plan C, how much Ringer''s lactate should be given in the FIRST hour?', '["300 mL (30 mL/kg)", "1,000 mL (100 mL/kg)", "700 mL (70 mL/kg)", "200 mL (20 mL/kg)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Plan C: 100 mL/kg Ringer''s lactate in total. Infants under 12 months get 30 mL/kg in the first 1 hour, then 70 mL/kg over 5 hours; older children get 30 mL/kg in 30 minutes, then 70 mL/kg over 2½ hours. Reassess often, and start ORS as soon as the child can drink.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(178, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 15 kg, 4-year-old child has severe malaria with convulsions and coma. The correct dose of IV artesunate is:', '["60 mg (4 mg/kg)", "45 mg (3 mg/kg)", "36 mg (2.4 mg/kg)", "30 mg (2 mg/kg)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'WHO recommends 3 mg/kg of IV/IM artesunate for children under 20 kg (2.4 mg/kg for those 20 kg and above), at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then daily until oral therapy is possible. 15 × 3 = 45 mg. Also check and correct glucose, treat seizures, and transfuse if Hb is below 5 g/dL.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(179, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 2-year-old with sickle cell anaemia suddenly becomes pale and lethargic, with HR 170 and BP 70/40 mmHg. The spleen, previously not palpable, is now enlarged to the umbilicus. Hb has fallen from 8 to 4 g/dL, and the reticulocyte count is HIGH. The diagnosis is:', '["Acute chest syndrome", "Vaso-occlusive (painful) crisis", "Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19", "Acute splenic sequestration crisis"]'::jsonb, 3, 'In young children with sickle cell disease, sickled cells can suddenly pool in the spleen, causing rapid splenic enlargement, a sharp fall in Hb and hypovolaemic shock — but the bone marrow still responds (high reticulocytes). An aplastic crisis has a LOW reticulocyte count and no splenomegaly. Treat with urgent fluids and blood transfusion.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(180, 'Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care', 'A 6-week-old baby has had projectile, non-bilious vomiting after every feed for a week and is hungry straight after vomiting. A small olive-sized mass is felt in the epigastrium during a test feed. Blood gas shows pH 7.55, Cl⁻ 85 mmol/L and K⁺ 2.9 mmol/L. Before surgery, the priority is:', '["Take the baby for pyloromyotomy within the hour, before the alkalosis worsens", "Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the acid–base disturbance before surgery", "Restart small frequent oral feeds to replace the losses, then operate tomorrow", "Correct the fluid deficit and alkalosis with IV saline, dextrose and KCl first"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Pyloric stenosis is NOT a surgical emergency: the alkalosis must be corrected first (anaesthesia with alkalosis risks post-operative apnoea). Keep nil by mouth with an NG tube, and give IV 0.45% or 0.9% saline with 5–10% dextrose and added KCl until chloride and bicarbonate are normal. Bicarbonate would worsen the alkalosis.', 'Obstetric & Neonatal'),
(181, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A patient with 50% burns sustained 3 days ago needs rapid sequence intubation. Which drug should be AVOIDED?', '["Suxamethonium", "Fentanyl", "Ketamine", "Rocuronium"]'::jsonb, 0, 'From about 24–72 hours after burns, crush injury, spinal cord injury or prolonged immobility, muscle develops extra acetylcholine receptors. Suxamethonium can then cause massive potassium release and cardiac arrest. Use rocuronium (reversible with sugammadex).', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(182, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Sugammadex rapidly reverses neuromuscular blockade caused by:', '["Suxamethonium", "All neuromuscular blocking drugs", "Rocuronium and vecuronium", "Atracurium and cisatracurium"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Sugammadex is a cyclodextrin that encapsulates aminosteroid blockers (rocuronium, and to a lesser extent vecuronium). It does not work on benzylisoquinoliniums (atracurium, cisatracurium), which are reversed with neostigmine plus glycopyrrolate, or on suxamethonium.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(183, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A hypotensive patient (BP 80/50 mmHg) with acute severe asthma needs intubation. The most suitable induction agent is:', '["Midazolam in high dose", "Ketamine", "Thiopental", "Propofol"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Ketamine is sympathomimetic, so it supports BP and heart rate, and it is a bronchodilator; it largely preserves airway reflexes and respiratory drive. Propofol and thiopental cause vasodilation and myocardial depression, worsening hypotension. Etomidate is cardiostable but has no bronchodilator effect.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(184, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A young man with head injury has been sedated with high-dose propofol (above 5 mg/kg/h) for 4 days. He develops a worsening metabolic acidosis, rising CK and potassium, new bradyarrhythmias, rising triglycerides and cloudy (lipaemic) blood. The most likely diagnosis is:', '["Malignant hyperthermia", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Refeeding syndrome", "Propofol infusion syndrome"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Propofol infusion syndrome is linked to high doses (above 4 mg/kg/h) for over 48 hours, especially with catecholamines or steroids. It causes metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, hyperkalaemia, hypertriglyceridaemia, AKI, arrhythmias and cardiac failure. Stop propofol immediately and give supportive care (possibly haemofiltration).', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(185, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Minutes after a large dose of bupivacaine for a nerve block, a patient complains of a metallic taste, tingling around the mouth and tinnitus, then has a seizure followed by ventricular arrhythmias. The specific treatment is:', '["IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg", "IV naloxone 400 micrograms", "IV flumazenil 200 micrograms", "IV 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This is local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST): CNS excitation, then cardiovascular collapse. Give lipid emulsion, which acts as a ''lipid sink'', along with airway management, benzodiazepines for seizures and prolonged CPR if needed. Avoid lidocaine as an antiarrhythmic, and reduce adrenaline doses.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(186, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Shortly after a spinal anaesthetic for caesarean section, a woman becomes profoundly hypotensive and bradycardic, cannot move her arms, says she cannot breathe, then loses consciousness and stops breathing. The most likely cause is:', '["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity", "Amniotic fluid embolism", "Vasovagal reaction", "High (total) spinal block"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Excessive cranial spread of spinal anaesthetic blocks the sympathetic chain (hypotension, and bradycardia from cardioaccelerator fibres at T1–T4), the arms (C5–T1) and the phrenic nerve (C3–5), causing apnoea and unconsciousness. Treat with airway support and ventilation, vasopressors (ephedrine or phenylephrine), atropine, fluids and left uterine displacement.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(187, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A patient remains in septic shock (MAP 60 mmHg) on noradrenaline 0.4 micrograms/kg/min after adequate fluids. The Surviving Sepsis Campaign (2021) recommends next:', '["Adding vasopressin rather than increasing noradrenaline further", "Adding phenylephrine as the second agent", "Switching from noradrenaline to dopamine", "Giving a further 60 mL/kg of fluid regardless of response"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Noradrenaline is first-line; when the dose reaches about 0.25–0.5 mcg/kg/min, adding vasopressin (up to 0.03 units/min) is suggested. Dopamine causes more arrhythmias. If cardiac output is also low, adding dobutamine (or switching to adrenaline) is considered; IV hydrocortisone is suggested for ongoing vasopressor need.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(188, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Which inotrope increases myocardial contractility mainly through β1 stimulation, but may LOWER blood pressure through β2-mediated vasodilation?', '["Noradrenaline", "Phenylephrine", "Vasopressin", "Dobutamine"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dobutamine is used in low-output cardiogenic states to raise cardiac output. Its β2 vasodilator effect can drop BP, so it is often combined with noradrenaline in hypotensive patients. Noradrenaline (mainly α1) and phenylephrine (pure α1) constrict vessels; vasopressin acts on V1 receptors.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(189, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Which of the following is NOT a recognised adverse effect of long-term amiodarone?', '["Hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism", "Hyperkalaemia", "Slate-grey skin discolouration and photosensitivity", "Pulmonary fibrosis"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Amiodarone (rich in iodine, very long half-life) can cause thyroid dysfunction, pulmonary fibrosis, liver toxicity, corneal deposits, optic neuropathy, photosensitivity, grey skin, peripheral neuropathy and QT prolongation. Hyperkalaemia is not a feature. IV amiodarone should go through a central line where possible (phlebitis).', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(190, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A patient on dabigatran has a life-threatening intracranial bleed. The specific reversal agent is:', '["Idarucizumab", "Andexanet alfa", "Vitamin K with prothrombin complex concentrate", "Protamine sulphate"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Idarucizumab is a monoclonal antibody fragment that binds dabigatran (a direct thrombin inhibitor). Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors (apixaban, rivaroxaban). Protamine reverses unfractionated heparin (and partly LMWH). Vitamin K plus PCC reverses warfarin.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(191, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A patient given IV naloxone for a methadone overdose wakes up fully. The main nursing concern over the next few hours is that:', '["Once fully awake after naloxone, the patient needs no further observation", "Naloxone itself causes a long-lasting respiratory depression after waking", "Naloxone must never be repeated because further doses cause toxicity", "Naloxone wears off before methadone, so respiratory depression can return"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Many opioids, especially methadone and sustained-release preparations, outlast naloxone. Watch sedation level and respiratory rate closely, and repeat doses or start an infusion if needed. Naloxone can also cause acute withdrawal (agitation, vomiting, pulmonary oedema).', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(192, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'To reduce nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity with once-daily gentamicin, the dosing interval is adjusted mainly according to:', '["The urine volume passed in the last hour", "The peak level 1 hour after the dose", "The trough (pre-dose) or nomogram level", "The patient''s daily temperature chart"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Gentamicin kills bacteria in proportion to its peak concentration, but toxicity relates to sustained exposure (high troughs). Levels are used to lengthen the dosing interval when clearance is reduced (renal impairment, elderly). Avoid combining it with other nephrotoxins and loop diuretics.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(193, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'During a rapid IV vancomycin infusion, a patient develops flushing, itching and a red rash on the face, neck and upper chest, with mild hypotension. There is no wheeze or airway swelling. The correct response is:', '["Anaphylaxis — give IM adrenaline and never use vancomycin again", "Infusion (''red man'') reaction — pause, give an antihistamine, restart slower", "Serum sickness — stop the drug and start a course of oral steroids", "Stevens–Johnson syndrome — stop all drugs and refer to dermatology"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Vancomycin given too fast releases histamine directly (not IgE-mediated): flushing of the upper body, itching and sometimes hypotension. Infuse no faster than about 1 g per hour (or 10 mg/min). True anaphylaxis (wheeze, angioedema, collapse) needs adrenaline.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(194, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'An ICU nurse promises a frightened patient that she will be the one to update his wife after the procedure, and makes sure she does so even though the shift is busy. Which ethical principle does this show?', '["Veracity", "Non-maleficence", "Fidelity", "Justice"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Fidelity is faithfulness to commitments and promises, which builds trust. Veracity is truth-telling; justice is fairness in distributing care and resources; non-maleficence is avoiding harm.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(195, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'In a negligence claim against a nurse, the element proved by showing that the patient''s injury would NOT have happened ''but for'' the nurse''s failure is:', '["Damage (harm)", "Breach of duty", "Duty of care", "Causation"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Negligence needs all four elements: a duty of care, a breach of that duty (falling below the standard of a reasonable nurse), causation (the breach caused the harm — the ''but for'' test) and damage. Without causation there is no liability, even if care fell below the standard.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(196, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'An unconscious trauma patient with no relatives present needs an emergency life-saving procedure. Treatment can proceed legally on the basis of:', '["Written informed consent signed by the nurse in charge", "A court order, which must be obtained before any treatment", "Proxy consent from any staff member on duty", "The doctrine of necessity (implied consent) in an emergency"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When a patient lacks capacity and treatment is urgent and in their best interests, it may be given without consent under the emergency doctrine of necessity — unless there is a known valid advance refusal. Staff cannot sign consent on a patient''s behalf. Document the reasons clearly.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(197, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'After caring for a patient with Clostridioides difficile diarrhoea, the correct hand hygiene is:', '["Use a chlorhexidine–alcohol hand rub", "Wash hands with soap and running water", "Use alcohol-based hand rub only", "Wearing gloves is enough; hand hygiene is not needed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Alcohol does not kill C. difficile spores; mechanical washing with soap and water removes them. Use contact precautions (gloves and apron or gown), a single room with its own toilet, and sporicidal (chlorine-based) cleaning of the environment.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(198, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'A nurse gets a deep needlestick injury from a patient known to be HIV-positive. After washing and encouraging bleeding of the wound, post-exposure prophylaxis should be started:', '["As soon as possible (no later than 72 h), for 28 days", "After her own HIV test result returns, for 28 days", "Within 24 hours of the injury, taken for 6 months", "Within 7 days of the injury, taken for 14 days"]'::jsonb, 0, 'PEP works best the earlier it starts, and it is not recommended after 72 hours. The standard course is 28 days of combination antiretrovirals, with baseline and follow-up HIV, hepatitis B and C testing, and incident reporting. Hepatitis B vaccination or immunoglobulin is given depending on immune status.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(199, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'Which of the following is NOT one of the ''five rights'' of delegation?', '["The right task", "The right person", "The right supervision", "The right cost"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The five rights of delegation are: right task, right circumstances, right person, right direction/communication, and right supervision/evaluation. The registered nurse remains accountable for the decision to delegate and for supervising the outcome.', 'Ethics, Law & Management'),
(200, 'Pharmacology, Anaesthesia and Professional Practice', 'In the hierarchy of evidence for evidence-based practice, the strongest evidence on the effectiveness of an intervention usually comes from:', '["A large prospective cohort study followed for years", "Expert opinion from a respected professional body", "A systematic review and meta-analysis of RCTs", "A single large multicentre randomised controlled trial"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A systematic review with meta-analysis of high-quality RCTs pools results and minimises bias and chance, so it sits at the top of the hierarchy. Next come individual RCTs, then cohort and case-control studies, case series, and expert opinion at the bottom.', 'Ethics, Law & Management');
